overall mathematical structure more carefully, because I want to make sure the approach is actually correct before investing in formalization.\n\n**The mathematical setup:**\n\nWe want to show `∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ ≥ 0` where:\n- `G(U) = f(U) · exp(-β·S⁺(U)) · exp(-β·S_int(U)/2)` (the OS Boltzmann factor, with f depending only on pos+interface links)\n- θ is time reflection.\n\nEquivalently (via `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`): `⟨g, Tg⟩ ≥ 0` where g is the restriction of f to pos+interface, and T is the transfer matrix.\n\n**The transfer matrix:**\n`(Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺)/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)`\n\nWait, let me re-read the transfer matrix definition. Let me look at `transferMatrixReflected`.\n\nActually, let me think about the cleanest approach. The §8.11.32 approach is quite involved. Let me reconsider whether there's a simpler path.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The key mathematical fact is:\n\n**Reflection positivity for lattice gauge theory** (Osterwalder-Seiler): The integral `∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ ≥ 0` where G is the OS Boltzmann factor.\n\nThe standard proof (Osterwalder-Seiler, also in Seiler's book and Glimm-Jaffe) goes **plaquette by plaquette**. The idea is:\n\nThe Boltzmann factor is a product over plaquettes: `exp(-β·S) = ∏_p exp(c·Re Tr(U_p))`. Each plaquette factor `exp(c·Re Tr(U_p))` is a PD function on SU(N)⁴ (the four links of the plaquette). \n\nReflection positivity then follows from the fact that:\n1. Each plaquette factor is PD.\n2. The product of PD functions is PD (in a suitable sense).\n3. The reflection maps plaquettes to plaquettes, and the \"reflected\" plaquette factor pairs up correctly.\n\nThe cleanest formalization might actually be the **plaquette-by-plaquette induction** (approach 4 in §8.11.31), NOT the character expansion approach.\n\nBut wait — the project has invested heavily in the character expansion approach (Lemma 3, the fullReflect form, etc.). Let me thi