tandard proof.\n\nThe standard proof of reflection positivity (Osterwalder-Seiler) works as follows:\n\nConsider the integral `I = ∫ f(U)·f(θU) · ∏_p φ_p(U) dμ`.\n\nWe integrate out the links one \"layer\" at a time, starting from the links farthest from the interface.\n\nActually, the cleanest version I know is:\n\n**Theorem (Reflection Positivity):** If each plaquette factor `φ_p` is PD, then `∫ f(U)·f(θU) · ∏_p φ_p(U) dμ ≥ 0` for all f depending on pos+interface links.\n\n**Proof sketch (plaquette by plaquette):** \n\nThe key is to integrate out the temporal links at the interface (the w links) FIRST, using the PD property. Then the remaining integral is a product of PD factors that can be handled by induction.\n\nHmm, actually, let me think about this more carefully. The standard proof for the Wilson action specifically:\n\nThe Wilson action plaquettes can be grouped as:\n1. **Positive plaquettes** (entirely in the positive time region): depend only on positive links.\n2. **Negative plaquettes** (entirely in the negative time region): depend only on negative links.\n3. **Interface plaquettes** (straddling time 0): depend on pos, neg, and interface links.\n\nThe integral is:\n`I = ∫ f(U)·f(θU) · [∏_{P⁺} φ_p] · [∏_{P⁻} φ_p] · [∏_{P⁰} φ_p] dμ`\n\nNow, `f(U)` depends on pos+interface, `f(θU)` depends on neg+interface (after reflection). The positive plaquette factors depend on pos links, negative on neg links.\n\nThe interface plaquettes are the hard part. They couple pos, neg, and interface.\n\nThe decomposition from §8.11.32:\n`S_int = S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) + S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰_s)`\n\nSo `∏_{P⁰} φ_p = exp(-β·S_spatial_0) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower)`.\n\nNow:\n- `exp(-β·S_spatial_0)` depends on u⁰_s only.\n- `exp(-β·S_ts_upper)` depends on (u⁺, u⁰_s, w).\n- `exp(-β·S_ts_lower)` depends on (U⁻, u⁰_s).\n\nAnd `f(U) = f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)`, `f(θU) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹)`.\n\nSo:\n`I = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp