egral is:\n\n`I = ∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·exp(c·Re Tr(w·A_x))·[other factors] dw·dA_x·...`\n\nwhere A_x involves positive and spatial interface links. If we integrate out w first:\n\n`∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·exp(c·Re Tr(w·A_x)) dw`\n\nThis is a single-link reflection positivity integral. The function `exp(c·Re Tr(w·A_x))` as a function of w (with A_x fixed) is PD (it's a plaquette factor, PD on SU(N)). And `f(w)·f(w⁻¹)` is the reflection structure.\n\nBut as noted in §8.11.31 Finding 3, `∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·K(w) dw` is NOT necessarily ≥ 0 for a single PD function K. The PD property gives `∫∫ f(x)·conj(f(y))·K(x⁻¹y) dx dy ≥ 0`, which is a DOUBLE integral, not the single integral `∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·K(w) dw`.\n\nSo even for a single link, the PD property alone is insufficient. The non-negativity comes from the SPECIFIC structure of the plaquette factor and the fact that we're integrating over ALL links (not just w).\n\nHmm, so the single-link case is already nontrivial. Let me think about why `∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·exp(c·Re Tr(w·A)) dw ≥ 0` for the plaquette factor.\n\nActually, wait. Is this even true? Let me think of a counterexample. Take f(w) = χ_λ(w) (a character). Then `∫ χ_λ(w)·χ_λ(w⁻¹)·exp(c·Re Tr(w·A)) dw = ∫ χ_λ(w)·conj(χ_λ(w))·exp(c·Re Tr(w·A)) dw = ∫ |χ_λ(w)|²·exp(c·Re Tr(w·A)) dw`. Since `|χ_λ|² ≥ 0` and `exp(c·Re Tr(w·A)) > 0` (for the Wilson action with c > 0... actually c = β²/N > 0, and Re Tr can be negative, but exp is always positive), this is ≥ 0. ✓\n\nBut for a general f, `f(w)·f(w⁻¹)` is not necessarily ≥ 0. For example, f(w) = χ_λ(w) + i·χ_μ(w) (complex). Then `f(w)·f(w⁻¹) = (χ_λ(w) + i·χ_μ(w))·(conj(χ_λ(w)) + i·conj(χ_μ(w)))`... hmm, but f is REAL in our case (f : ... → ℝ). So f(w)·f(w⁻¹) is real but not necessarily ≥ 0.\n\nWait, but f is real. So `f(w)·f(w⁻¹)` is real. And `exp(c·Re Tr(w·A))` is real and positive. So the integral is real. But is it ≥ 0?\n\nFor f real, `∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·K(w) dw` where K is PD. Let me expand f in Peter-Weyl: `f(w) = ∑_λ ∑_{i,j} c_{λ,i,j}·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}`. Then `f(w⁻¹) = ∑_λ ∑_{i,j} c_{λ,i,j}·(ρ_λ(w⁻¹))_{ij} = ∑_λ ∑_{i,j} c_{λ,i,j}·conj((ρ_λ(w))_{ji})` (since ρ(w⁻¹) = ρ(w)†).\n\nSo `f(w)·f(w⁻¹) = ∑_{λ,i,j} ∑_{μ,k,l} c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})`.\n\nAnd `∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·K(w) dw = ∑_{λ,i,j} ∑_{μ,k,l} c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}·∫ (ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})·K(w) dw`.\n\nNow, K(w) = exp(c·Re Tr(w·A)) is PD, so it has a character expansion `K(w) = ∑_ν a_ν·χ_ν(w)` with `a_ν ≥ 0` (for the plaquette factor, this is the Peter-Weyl / Clebsch-Gordan expansion).\n\nSo `∫ (ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})·K(w) dw = ∑_ν a_ν·∫ (ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})·χ_ν(w) dw`.\n\nAnd `∫ (ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})·χ_ν(w) dw` is EXACTLY the triple product integral! By `triple_product_character_matrix_integral`, this equals `(1/dims μ)·∑_a cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,l)·conj(cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,j,k))`.\n\nSo `∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·K(w) dw = ∑_ν a_ν·∑_{λ,i,j}∑_{μ,k,l} c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}·(1/dims μ)·∑_a cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,l)·conj(cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,j,k))`.\n\nNow, define `D_ν(a) = ∑_{λ,i,j} c_{λ,i,j}·cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,l)·...`. Hmm, this is getting complicated because λ and μ are coupled through the CG coefficients.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The triple product integral gives:\n`∫ χ_ν(w)·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{kl}) dw = (1/dims μ)·∑_a cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,k)·conj(cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,j,l))`.\n\nThis is a Gram matrix in (a, i, k) vs (a, j, l). So:\n\n`∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·K(w) dw = ∑_ν a_ν·(1/dims μ)·∑_{λ,μ}∑_{i,j,k,l} c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}·∑_a cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,k)·conj(cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,j,l))`.\n\nHmm, but the issue is that `c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}` is NOT of the form `d(x)·conj(d(y))` because both c's are real (f is real) but they come from DIFFERENT representations λ and μ. The Gram matrix structure would need `c_{λ,i,j}` to pair with `conj(c_{λ,i,j})` (same representation), but here we have `c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}` (different representations).\n\nWait, but f is REAL. So `c_{μ,k,l}` for the expansion of f(w⁻¹)... Actually, let me reconsider. f(w) = ∑ c_{λ,i,j}·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}. f(w⁻¹) = ∑ c_{λ,i,j}·(ρ_λ(w⁻¹))_{ij} = ∑ c_{λ,i,j}·conj((ρ_λ(w))_{ji}).\n\nSo `f(w)·f(w⁻¹) = [∑_λ ∑_{i,j} c_{λ,i,j}·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}]·[∑_μ ∑_{k,l} c_{μ,k,l}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})]`.\n\nThe integral is:\n`∫ f(w)·f(w⁻¹)·K(w) dw = ∑_ν a_ν ∑_λ ∑_μ ∑_{i,j} ∑_{k,l} c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}·∫ χ_ν(w)·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk}) dw`.\n\nUsing the triple product:\n`= ∑_ν a_ν ∑_λ ∑_μ ∑_{i,j} ∑_{k,l} c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}·(1/dims μ)·∑_a cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,l)·conj(cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,j,k))`.\n\nHmm wait, I need to be careful about the index matching. The triple product is `∫ χ_ν(w)·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{kl}) dw`. In our case, the conj part is `conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})`, so k↔l are swapped. So:\n\n`∫ χ_ν(w)·(ρ_λ(w))_{ij}·conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk}) dw = (1/dims μ)·∑_a cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,l)·conj(cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,j,k))`.\n\nSo the full expression is:\n`∑_ν a_ν·(1/dims μ)·∑_a |∑_λ ∑_{i,j} ∑_μ ∑_{k,l} ...|²`?\n\nNo, it's not a simple square because λ and μ are summed independently. Let me think again.\n\nActually, let me define:\n`D_ν(a) = ∑_λ ∑_{i,j} ∑_μ ∑_{k,l} c_{λ,i,j}·c_{μ,k,l}·cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,l)`.\n\nHmm, but then `conj(D_ν(a)) = ∑ conj(c_{λ,i,j})·conj(c_{μ,k,l})·conj(cgME(ν,λ,μ,a,i,l))`. Since f is real, `conj(c_{λ,i,j})` is NOT simply related to `c_{λ,i,j}` (the reality constraint is `c_{dual(λ),j,i} = conj(c_{λ,i,j})`, not `conj(c_{λ,i,j}) = c_{λ,i,j}`).\n\nSo `D_ν(a)·conj(D_ν(a))` would involve `c·conj(c)` which is NOT the same as `c·c` (what we have). So this is NOT a norm squared.\n\nThis confirms §8.11.31 Finding 3: for a single link, the integral is an inner product, not a norm squared, and is NOT necessarily ≥ 0.\n\nBUT — and this is the key — the integral is over ALL links, not just w. The other links (A_x, etc.) provide additional structure. Specifically, A_x depends on positive links, and when we integrate over the positive links too, the PD property of the positive plaquette factors kicks in.\n\nSo the non-negativity is a JOINT property of all the integrals, not just the w-integral. This is why the