genuinely hard, and the project has been exploring different approaches. Let me focus on what's MOST LIKELY to succeed and is cleanest to formalize.\n\nLet me reconsider the §8.11.32 approach more carefully, because I think it might actually work if formalized correctly.\n\n**The §8.11.32 approach in detail:**\n\nThe integral (after the identity `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)`):\n`I = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ`\n\nDecompose: `S = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_spatial_0 + S_ts_upper + S_ts_lower`.\n\n`f(U) = f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)`, `f(θU) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹)`.\n\nChange variables V⁺ = reflect(U⁻):\n`I = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)·f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)·exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(u⁺,u⁰_s,w) + S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺),u⁰_s))) dμ⁺(u⁺)·dμ⁺(V⁺)·dμ⁰_s·dμ_w`\n\nNow, `S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰_s)`: the lower interface plaquettes involve negative temporal links (at time -1) and spatial interface links. Under reflection, time -1 → time +1, temporal links inverted. So the negative temporal links at time -1 become positive temporal links at time +1 (inverted). And the spatial interface links are unchanged. So `S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰_s)` becomes a function of V⁺ (positive links) and u⁰_s.\n\nBut what function? The lower plaquettes at time -1 become plaquettes at time +1. A plaquette at time -1 with directions (0, i) involves:\n- Temporal link at (time -1, x, 0): from time -1 to time 0.\n- Spatial link at (time -1, x, i).\n- Temporal link at (time -1, x+ê_i, 0): from time -1 to time 0.\n- Spatial link at (time 0, x, i) = u⁰_s.\n\nUnder reflection (time t → -t, temporal inverted):\n- Temporal link at (time -1, x, 0) → temporal link at (time 1, x, 0) INVERTED. This is a positive link (from time 1 to time 2).\n- Spatial link at (time -1, x, i) → spatial link at (time 1, x, i). Positive link.\n- Temporal link at (time -1, x+ê_i, 0) → temporal link at (time 1, x+ê_i, 0) INVERTED. Positive link.\n- Spatial link at (time 0, x, i) → spatial link at (time 0, x, i). Interface link u⁰_s.\n\nSo the reflected lower plaquette involves positive temporal links at time 1 (inverted) and spatial interface links u⁰_s. This is a plaquette at time 1 with directions (0, i), involving temporal links at time 1 and spatial links at time 0 and time 1.\n\nNow, is this the same as S_ts_upper? S_ts_upper is plaquettes at time 0 with directions (0, i), involving temporal links at time 0 (w) and spatial links at time 0 (u⁰_s) and time 1 (positive). The reflected lower plaquette is at time 1, involving temporal links at time 1 and spatial links at time 0 and time 1. These are DIFFERENT plaquettes!\n\nHmm, so `S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰_s)` is NOT `S_ts_upper(V⁺, u⁰_s, w)`. It's a different set of plaquettes.\n\nWait, but actually, the plaquette at time 1 with directions (0, i) — is this a positive plaquette or an interface plaquette? It involves temporal links at time 1 (positive) and spatial links at time 0 (interface) and time 1 (positive). Since it involves an interface link (u⁰_s at time 0), it IS an interface plaquette.\n\nBut wait, I thought the interface plaquettes were decomposed into S_spatial_0, S_ts_upper, and S_ts_lower. If the reflected S_ts_lower gives plaquettes at time 1, are those already counted in S_ts_upper?\n\nI think the issue is that I'm not correctly identifying the interface plaquettes. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think the interface plaquettes are those that involve at least one interface link (link at time 0). A plaquette at time 1 with directions (0, i) involves:\n- Temporal link at (time 1, x, 0): from time 1 to time 2. Positive.\n- Spatial link at (time 1, x, i). Positive.\n- Temporal link at (time 1, x+ê_i, 0): from time 1 to time 2. Positive.\n- Spatial link at (time 0, x, i) = u⁰_s. Interface.\n\nWait, does a plaquette at time 1 with directions (0, i) involve a spatial link at time 0? Let me think about the plaquette structure.\n\nA plaquette at base site (t, x) with directions (μ, ν) involves the four links:\n- U((t, x), μ)\n- U((t, x) + ê_μ, ν)\n- U((t, x) + ê_ν, μ)⁻¹\n- U((t, x), ν)⁻¹\n\nFor a plaquette at (1, x) with directions (0, i):\n- U((1, x), 0): temporal link at time 1, from time 1 to time 2. Positive.\n- U((1, x) + ê_0, i) = U((2, x), i): spatial link at time 2. Positive.\n- U((1, x) + ê_i, 0)⁻¹ = U((1, x+ê_i), 0)⁻¹: temporal link at time 1. Positive.\n- U((1, x), i)⁻¹: spatial link at time 1. Positive.\n\nSo a plaquette at (1, x) with directions (0, i) involves ONLY positive links (times 1 and 2). It does NOT involve interface links. So it's a POSITIVE plaquette, not an interface plaquette.\n\nHmm, so my earlier analysis was wrong. Let me reconsider what the reflected S_ts_lower looks like.\n\nThe lower interface plaquettes are at time -1 with directions (0, i):\n- U((-1, x), 0): temporal link at time -1, from time -1 to time 0. NEGATIVE.\n- U((-1, x) + ê_0, i) = U((0, x), i): spatial link at time 0. INTERFACE (u⁰_s).\n- U((-1, x) + ê_i, 0)⁻¹ = U((-1, x+ê_i), 0)⁻¹: temporal link at time -1. NEGATIVE.\n- U((-1, x), i)⁻¹: spatial link at time -1. NEGATIVE.\n\nUnder reflection (time t → -t, temporal inverted, spatial unchanged):\n- U((-1, x), 0) → U((1, x), 0)⁻¹: temporal link at time 1, INVERTED. POSITIVE.\n- U((0, x), i) → U((0, x), i): spatial link at time 0. INTERFACE (u⁰_s). UNCHANGED.\n- U((-1, x+ê_i), 0)⁻¹ → U((1, x+ê_i), 0): temporal link at time 1. POSITIVE. (The ⁻¹ and the reflection inversion cancel.)\n- U((-1, x), i)⁻¹ → U((1, x), i)⁻¹: spatial link at time 1, INVERTED. POSITIVE.\n\nSo the reflected lower plaquette involves: temporal link at time 1 (inverted), spatial interface link u⁰_s, temporal link at time 1, spatial link at time 1 (inverted).\n\nThis is a plaquette at time 1 with directions (0, i) but with the spatial link at time 0 (u⁰_s) involved. Wait, but I just showed that a plaquette at (1, x) with directions (0, i) involves links at times 1 and 2, not time 0. So the reflected lower plaquette is NOT a standard plaquette at (1, x).\n\nI think the issue is that the reflection of a plaquette is NOT a plaquette at the reflected base site with the same directions. The reflection maps the plaquette to a different plaquette.\n\nLet me reconsider. The reflection θ maps site (t, x) → (-t, x). A plaquette at base site (-1, x) with directions (0, i) has corners at:\n- (-1, x), (-1+1, x) = (0, x), (-1, x+ê_i), (0, x+ê_i).\n\nUnder θ: (-1, x) → (1, x), (0, x) → (0, x), (-1, x+ê_i) → (1, x+ê_i), (0, x+ê_i) → (0, x+ê_i).\n\nSo the reflected plaquette has corners at (1, x), (0, x), (1, x+ê_i), (0, x+ê_i). This is a plaquette with base site (0, x) and directions (0, i) (going from time 0 to time 1)! \n\nSo the reflected lower plaquette (at time -1) is an UPPER plaquette (at time 0)! And it involves:\n- Temporal link at (0, x, 0) = w(x): INTERFACE temporal.\n- Spatial link at (0, x, i) = u⁰_s(x): INTERFACE spatial.\n- Temporal link at (0, x+ê_i, 0) = w(x+ê_i): INTERFACE temporal.\n- Spatial link at (1, x, i): POSITIVE.\n\nWait, but that's exactly S_ts_upper! So `S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰_s) = S_ts_upper(V⁺, u⁰_s, w)` where w is the temporal interface link?\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety with the inversions. Let me be more careful.\n\nThe plaquette at (-1, x) with directions (0, i) is:\n`U_p = U((-1,x), 0) · U((0,x), i) · U((-1,x+ê_i), 0)⁻¹ · U((-1,x), i)⁻¹`\n\nUnder reflection θ (which maps U(n, μ) → U(θn, μ)⁻¹ if μ=0, else U(θn, μ)):\n- `U((-1,x), 0) → U((1,x), 0)⁻¹`\n- `U((0,x), i) → U((0,x), i)` (spatial, unchanged)\n- `U((-1,x+ê_i), 0)⁻¹ → U((1,x+ê_i), 0)` (the ⁻¹ from the plaquette and the ⁻¹ from reflection cancel)\n- `U((-1,x), i)⁻¹ → U((1,x), i)⁻¹` (spatial, unchanged, but the plaquette ⁻¹ remains)\n\nSo the reflected plaquette is:\n`θ(U_p) = U((1,x), 0)⁻¹ · U((0,x), i) · U((1,x+ê_i), 0) · U((1,x), i)⁻¹`\n\nNow, the upper plaquette at (0, x) with directions (0, i) is:\n`U_p' = U((0,x), 0) · U((1,x), i) · U((0,x+ê_i), 0)⁻¹ · U((0,x), i)⁻¹`\n`= w(x) · U((1,x), i) · w(x+ê_i)⁻¹ · u⁰_s(x)⁻¹`\n\nAnd the reflected lower plaquette is:\n`θ(U_p) = U((1,x), 0)⁻¹ · u⁰_s(x) · U((1,x+ê_i), 0) · U((1,x), i)⁻¹`\n\nThese are NOT the same! The upper plaquette `U_p'` involves w(x), w(x+ê_i), u⁰_s(x), and U((1,x), i). The reflected lower plaquette `θ(U_p)` involves U((1,x), 0), U((1,x+ê_i), 0), u⁰_s(x), and U((1,x), i). These involve DIFFERENT links!\n\nSo `S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰_s) ≠ S_ts_upper(V⁺, u⁰_s, w)` in general. The reflected lower plaquette involves positive TEMPORAL links (at time 1), while the upper plaquette involves positive SPATIAL links (at time 1) and temporal INTERFACE links (w).\n\nHmm, so the reflection of S_ts_lower is NOT S_ts_upper. They involve different links. This means the §8.11.32 Finding 4 claim that `B(u⁰_s) = A(u⁰_s)` is WRONG (as noted in Finding 5).\n\nOK so this is getting really complicated. Let me step back and think about the cleanest approach.\n\nActually, I realize I should look at the actual code to understand the plaquette structure and the action decomposition, rather than trying to work it out from first principles. Let me look at the interface action decomposition in the code.\n\nLet me search for the decomposition `S_int = S_spatial_0 + S_ts_upper + S_ts_lower` in the code.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole approach. I've been spending a lot of time on the math. Let me think about what's the most PRACTICAL path forward.\n\nThe project has been working on this for 27+ sessions. The character expansion approach (fullReflect form) is proven but the reorganization is stuck. The temporal expansion approach (§8.11.32) is promising but has the a_γ U⁺-dependence issue and the B ≠ A issue.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the cleanest path is to prove the identity `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)` and then use a DIFFERENT argument for the non-negativity.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the problem. The key difficulty is the interface plaquettes. Let me think about what happens if we integrate out the temporal interface links w FIRST.\n\nThe integral is:\n`I = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)·f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)·exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(u⁺,u⁰_s,w) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺,u⁰_s))) dμ⁺·dμ⁺·dμ⁰_s·dμ_w`\n\nwhere `S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)` is the reflected lower interface action (involving positive temporal links at time 1 and u⁰_s).\n\nNow, w appears in:\n- `f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)`: the first f-factor.\n- `f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)`: the second f-factor (with w⁻¹).\n- `S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)`: the upper interface action.\n\nAnd `S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)` does NOT involve w (it involves positive temporal links at time 1, which are part of V⁺, not w).\n\nSo the w-integral is:\n`∫ f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)·f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)·exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) dμ_w`\n\nThis is a single integral over w (the temporal interface links), with u⁺, V⁺, u⁰_s as parameters.\n\nNow, `S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)` is a sum over spatial sites x of plaquette contributions, each involving w(x), w(x+ê_i), u⁰_s(x), and positive spatial links at time 1. Since the temporal interface links w are at different spatial sites, and the plaquette at site x involves w(x) and w(x+ê_i), the w-integral does NOT factorize over sites x (because w(x) appears in plaquettes at both x and x-ê_i).\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider. The temporal interface links are `w(x) = U((0,x), 0)` for each spatial site x. The upper plaquette at (0, x) with directions (0, i) involves w(x) = U((0,x), 0) and w(x+ê_i) = U((0,x+ê_i), 0). So w(x) appears in the plaquette at (0, x) (as the first link) and in the plaquette at (0, x-ê_i) (as the third link, inverted). So the w's are coupled through the plaquettes.\n\nBut actually, for a given direction i, the plaquette at (0, x) with directions (0, i) involves w(x) and w(x+ê_i). If we fix i and look at all x, the w's form a chain: w(x) appears in plaquettes at x (with w(x+ê_i)) and at x-ê_i (with w(x)). So the w-integral is a 1D chain integral (for each direction i), not a product of independent integrals.\n\nThis is getting very complicated. The w-integral does NOT factorize simply.\n\nOK, I think I need to take a completely different approach to this problem. Let me reconsider.\n\n**The fundamental issue:** The reflection positivity integral `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ` is hard because the interface plaquettes couple positive, negative, and interface links in a complex way.\n\n**The standard proof (Osterwalder-Seiler):** The proof uses the fact that the Boltzmann factor is a product of PD plaquette factors, and applies a \"doubling\" trick. The key idea is:\n\n1. Write `exp(-β·S) = ∏_p φ_p` where each `φ_p = exp(c·Re Tr(U_p))` is PD.\n2. The reflection θ maps plaquettes to plaquettes: positive ↔ negative, interface → interface.\n3. Pair up the plaquettes: each positive plaquette p pairs with its reflection θp (negative). The interface plaquettes are self-paired.\n4. For each pair (p, θp), the product `φ_p(U)·φ_{θp}(U)` involves links from both sides of the interface.\n5. The PD property of each plaquette factor, combined with the pairing, gives the non-negativity.\n\nThe cleanest version of this argument that I know is:\n\n**Theorem:** Let `K(g, h) = φ(g·h⁻¹)` be a PD kernel on a group G (i.e., φ is PD). Then for any f, `∫∫ f(g)·conj(f(h))·K(g, h) dg dh = ∫ f(g)·conj(f(h))·φ(g·h⁻¹) dg dh ≥ 0`.\n\nThis is the standard PD quadratic form. But reflection positivity is `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·∏_p φ_p(U) dμ`, which is a SINGLE integral, not a double integral.\n\nThe connection: the single integral can be converted to a double integral by \"splitting\" the Boltzmann factor. Specifically:\n\n`∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·[∏_{P⁺} φ_p(U)]·[∏_{P⁻} φ_p(U)]·[∏_{P⁰} φ_p(U)] dμ`\n\nThe positive plaquette factors `∏_{P⁺} φ_p(U)` depend only on positive+interface links. The negative plaquette factors `∏_{P⁻} φ_p(U)` depend only on negative+interface links. By the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), the negative factors become `∏_{P⁺} φ_p(V⁺)` (positive plaquette factors of V⁺).\n\nAfter the change of variables:\n`I = ∫∫ f(u⁺, u⁰)·f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·[∏_{P⁺} φ_p(u⁺, u⁰)]·[∏_{P⁺} φ_p(V⁺, u⁰)]·[∏_{P⁰} φ_p(u⁺, V⁺, u⁰)] dμ⁺(u⁺)·dμ⁺(V⁺)·dμ⁰`\n\nwhere the interface plaquette factors `∏_{P⁰} φ_p` couple u⁺, V⁺, and u⁰.\n\nNow, the interface plaquettes involve temporal interface links w (which are inverted by σ) and spatial interface links u⁰_s (which are fixed by σ). The temporal links w appear in the upper plaquettes (coupling u⁺ and w) and the reflected lower plaquettes (coupling V⁺ and w... wait, no. The lower plaquettes don't involve w, they involve negative temporal links which become positive temporal links after reflection).\n\nHmm, I keep going in circles. Let me just look at the code to understand the exact structure.\n\nLet me look at the interface action decomposition and the plaquette structure in the code. Let me search for the relevant definitions.\n\nActually, let me take a completely different tack. Instead of trying to work out the math from scratch, let me look at what the LITERATURE says about the Osterwalder-Seiler proof, and see if there's a clean formulation I can use.\n\nLet me search for the Osterwalder-Seiler reflection positivity proof.\n\nActually, let me first check: is the identity `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)` already proven in the code? This would be a key simplification. Let me search for it.\n\nLet me also look at the `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` lemma to understand the current approach.\n\nLet me search the code for relevant lemmas.","turn":20,"is_prune_marker":false}
