{"id":49,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\TransferMatrix.lean\n-- 5709 lines, 276989 bytes --\n5370 |   intro V\n5371 |   dsimp only\n5372 |   rw [charFactorPos_thetaReindex_eq N T L hT ι dims ρ dual w V]\n5373 | \n5374 |   #print axioms fourierCoeffPos_thetaReindex_eq\n5375 | \n5376 | \n5377 | /-!\n5378 | ## Lemma 3 alternative: full reflection reindexing `w*` (swaps pos ↔ neg)\n5379 | \n5380 | The `thetaReindex` (θ) is a PROJECTION (idempotent, not a bijection), so the sum\n5381 | reindexing `w ↦ θw` is INVALID (see §8.11.24).  The CORRECT reflection symmetry\n5382 | uses the FULL reflection reindexing `w* = fullReflectReindex`, which swaps\n5383 | pos ↔ neg via `reflectInterfaceLink` (φ), applying `dual` on time-like links.\n5384 | \n5385 | `w*` is defined by:\n5386 | - `l ∈ pos`, time-like: `w*(l) = dual(w(φ(l)))`  (φ(l) ∈ neg, inverted link)\n5387 | - `l ∈ pos`, spatial:   `w*(l) = w(φ(l))`         (φ(l) ∈ neg, unchanged link)\n5388 | - `l ∈ int`:            `w*(l) = w(l)`             (unchanged)\n5389 | - `l ∈ neg`:            `w*(l) = w(l)`             (arbitrary, not used in identities)\n5390 | \n5391 | The key identity is `star(charFactorNeg dual w (reflectPosToNeg V⁺)) =\n5392 | charFactorPos (fullReflectReindex dual w) V⁺`, which gives\n5393 | `B_w(u⁰) = A_{w*}(σ(u⁰))` (the \"plain form\" relationship, without reindexing the sum).\n5394 | This is proved WITHOUT `dual` involutivity — the double `conj` cancels via `conj_conj`.\n5395 | See §8.11.25 for the full analysis.\n5396 | -/\n5397 | \n5398 | /-- The full reflection reindexing `w* : (InterfaceLink → ι) → (InterfaceLink → ι)`.\n5399 | For pos links, `w*` is determined by the neg link `φ(l)` (via `reflectInterfaceLink`),\n5400 | with `dual` applied on time-like links.  For int and neg links, `w*` is the identity.\n5401 | This is the reindexing that makes `star(charFactorNeg dual w (reflectPosToNeg V⁺)) =\n5402 | charFactorPos (fullReflectReindex dual w) V⁺` hold (Lemma 3 alternative). -/\n5403 | noncomputable def fullReflectReindex (T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T)\n5404 |     (ι : Type) (dual : ι → ι) (w : InterfaceLink T L → ι) :\n5405 |     InterfaceLink T L → ι :=\n5406 |   fun l =>\n5407 |     if hl_pos : l ∈ interfaceLinkPos T L then\n5408 |       if hμ : l.val.2 = 0 then dual (w (reflectInterfaceLink T L hT l))\n5409 |       else w (reflectInterfaceLink T L hT l)\n5410 |     else w l\n5411 | \n5412 | #print axioms fullReflectReindex\n5413 | \n5414 | /-- For a positive-time interface link `l` with `μ(l) = 0` (time-like),\n5415 | `w*(l) = dual(w(φ(l)))`. -/\n5416 | lemma fullReflectReindex_pos_time (T L : ℕ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L] (hT : Odd T)\n5417 |     (ι : Type) (dual : ι → ι) (w : InterfaceLink T L → ι)\n5418 |     {l : InterfaceLink T L} (hl : l ∈ interfaceLinkPos T L) (hμ : l.val.2 = 0) :\n5419 |     fullReflectReindex T L hT ι dual w l = dual (w (reflectInterfaceLink T L hT l)) := by\n5420 |   show (if hl_pos : l ∈ interfaceLinkPos T L then\n5421 |         if hμ : l.val.2 = 0 then dual (w (reflectInterfaceLink T L hT l))\n5422 |         else w (reflectInterfaceLink T L hT l)\n5423 |       else w l) = dual (w (reflectInterfaceLink T L hT l))\n5424 |   rw [dif_pos hl, dif_pos hμ]\n5425 | \n5426 | #print axioms fullReflectReindex_pos_time\n5427 | \n5428 | /-- For a positive-time interface link `l` with `μ(l) ≠ 0` (spatial),\n5429 | `w*(l) = w(φ(l))`. -/\n\n... 280 more line(s) below (use offset=5430 to continue reading)\nTime: 2026-08-05 08:10:52 UTC","timestamp":1785917452,"token_count":1067,"tool_call_id":"07285032-21f9-4048-85e4-f51f5d37d360","full_token_estimate":721,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":5709,"byte_count":276989,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":24,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":50,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1785917592,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"query\":\"Osterwalder Seiler reflection positivity lattice gauge theory transfer matrix proof\",\"max_results\":8,\"source\":\"auto\"}","name":"search_literature"},"id":"7610fccd-c8a8-48c6-8c83-2737920fb3fe","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":2144,"reasoning_content":"OK so `fullReflectReindex` (w*) is defined as:\n- For pos links, time-like (μ=0): `w*(l) = dual(w(φ(l)))` where φ = reflectInterfaceLink maps pos→neg.\n- For pos links, spatial (μ≠0): `w*(l) = w(φ(l))`.\n- For int and neg links: `w*(l) = w(l)` (identity).\n\nSo w* is NOT simply `dual ∘ w`. It's a reindexing that swaps pos↔neg via φ, with dual on time-like links.\n\nNow, the key identity from Lemma 3 is:\n`star(charFactorNeg dual w (reflectPosToNeg V⁺)) = charFactorPos (fullReflectReindex dual w) V⁺`\n\nAnd the fullReflect form is:\n`C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) · A_w(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n\nwhere `A_w(u⁰) = fourierCoeffPos(w, u⁰)` and `A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) = fourierCoeffPos(w*, σ(u⁰))`.\n\nNow, the question is: can we show this is ≥ 0?\n\nLet me think about this differently. The fullReflect form came from the character expansion of the INTERFACE Boltzmann factor. But the interface Boltzmann factor `exp(-β·S_int)` is a product of PD plaquette factors, so its character expansion has non-negative coefficients `F(w) ≥ 0`.\n\nThe integral `∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) · A_w(u⁰) dμ⁰` is over the interface links u⁰. The `charFactorInt(w, u⁰)` is the character factor of the interface links.\n\nNow, `A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(mergePosInterface(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) dμ⁺`.\n\nAnd `A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w*, V⁺) dμ⁺`.\n\nSo the full expression is:\n`C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{V⁺} ∫_{W⁺} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · charFactorPos(w*, V⁺) · charFactorPos(w, W⁺) · ψ(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · ψ(mergePosInterface(W⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(W⁺)/2) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺(W⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nThis is a triple integral. The character factors `charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · charFactorPos(w*, V⁺) · charFactorPos(w, W⁺)` involve the weight w at interface, positive (w*), and positive (w) links.\n\nNow, the key insight: the character factors are products of `χ_{w(l)}(link_value)` over all links. The full product `charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · charFactorPos(w*, V⁺) · charFactorPos(w, W⁺)` is a product over ALL positive and interface links (the negative links are not involved because charFactorNeg was already converted to charFactorPos via the fullReflect identity).\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. The character expansion `h_char` expands `exp(-β·S_int)` as:\n`exp(-β·S_int) = C · ∑_w F(w) · charFactorPos(w) · charFactorInt(w) · star(charFactorNeg(dual∘w))`\n\nAnd the fullReflect identity converts `star(charFactorNeg(dual∘w)(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺))) = charFactorPos(w*)(V⁺)`.\n\nSo after the fullReflect substitution, the character factors are:\n`charFactorPos(w)(W⁺) · charFactorInt(w)(u⁰) · charFactorPos(w*)(V⁺)`\n\nwhere W⁺ is the positive config in the first f-factor, V⁺ is the positive config in the second f-factor (after the change of variables), u⁰ is the interface config.\n\nNow, `charFactorPos(w)(W⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w(l)}(W⁺(l))` and `charFactorPos(w*)(V⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w*(l)}(V⁺(l))`.\n\nAnd `charFactorInt(w)(u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ int} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰(l))`.\n\nSo the full character factor is:\n`∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w(l)}(W⁺(l)) · ∏_{l ∈ int} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰(l)) · ∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w*(l)}(V⁺(l))`\n\nNow, w*(l) for pos links is `dual(w(φ(l)))` if time-like, `w(φ(l))` if spatial. And w*(l) for int links is `w(l)`.\n\nSo `∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w*(l)}(V⁺(l)) = ∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{...}(V⁺(l))` where the weight at l depends on w(φ(l)) (the neg link).\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about whether there's a way to see the full expression as a Gram matrix.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The full expression (after substituting the Fourier coefficients) is:\n\n`C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰, V⁺, W⁺} [∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w(l)}(W⁺(l))] · [∏_{l ∈ int} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰(l))] · [∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w*(l)}(V⁺(l))] · ψ(mergePosInterface(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · ψ(mergePosInterface(W⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(W⁺)/2)`\n\nNow, the character factor `∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w(l)}(W⁺(l)) · ∏_{l ∈ int} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰(l))` is a product over pos+int links of `χ_{w(l)}(link(l))`. This is a CHARACTER of the product group SU(N)^{pos+int} evaluated at the config (W⁺, u⁰).\n\nAnd `∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w*(l)}(V⁺(l))` is a product over pos links of `χ_{w*(l)}(V⁺(l))`. But w*(l) for pos links depends on w(φ(l)) (neg links), not w(l). So this is NOT a character of the same product group.\n\nHmm, this is the fundamental issue. The two character factors are at DIFFERENT weights (w vs w*) and involve DIFFERENT links (W⁺ vs V⁺). So the expression is NOT a standard Gram matrix.\n\nOK, I think I've been going around in circles. Let me take a completely different approach. Let me look at the LITERATURE for the Osterwalder-Seiler proof and see if there's a clean formulation.\n\nLet me search for it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the problem from the very beginning. The key identity is:\n\n`∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ`\n\nwhere `S = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int` is the full Wilson action, and `exp(-β·S) = ∏_p φ_p` is a product of PD plaquette factors.\n\nNow, the standard proof of reflection positivity for the Wilson action (Osterwalder-Seiler) is:\n\n**Theorem:** `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·∏_p φ_p(U) dμ ≥ 0` for all f depending on pos+interface links, where each `φ_p` is PD.\n\n**Proof:** The proof proceeds by integrating out the links in a specific order, using the PD property at each step. The key steps are:\n\n1. **Integrate out the negative links.** The negative plaquette factors depend only on negative links. After integrating out the negative links, we get a function of the positive and interface links. By the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), this becomes a function of V⁺ (positive) and u⁰ (interface).\n\n2. **The result is a \"transfer matrix\" form.** After integrating out the negative links, the integral becomes `∫ g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰` where T is the transfer matrix.\n\n3. **Show T is positive.** This is the hard part. T = B*·B where B is the Fourier coefficient extraction operator.\n\nThis is exactly the approach that's been formalized! The transfer matrix approach. And the hard part is showing T is positive, which is what we're stuck on.\n\nSo the question is: how does the standard proof show T is positive?\n\nThe standard proof (Osterwalder-Seiler, Seiler's book) shows T is positive by:\n\n**The plaquette factor is PD** → **the transfer matrix is a product of positive operators** → **T is positive**.\n\nThe key is that the transfer matrix can be decomposed as a product of \"one-plaquette transfer matrices\", each of which is positive (because the plaquette factor is PD). The product of positive operators is positive (if they commute, or more generally, if the product is of the form B*·B).\n\nActually, the Lüscher decomposition is exactly this: T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} where V is a positive multiplication operator and U is a positive integral operator. This gives T ≥ 0.\n\nBut the formalization of this is hard because:\n1. The plaquette factors couple multiple links.\n2. The transfer matrix involves an integral over V⁺.\n3. The PD property needs to be applied carefully.\n\nLet me think about whether there's a cleaner abstract formulation.\n\n**Abstract formulation:** Let G be a compact group (SU(N)^{links}), μ the Haar measure, θ a reflection (involution). Let K = ∏_p φ_p be a product of PD functions. Then `∫ f·f∘θ·K dμ ≥ 0` for all f depending on the \"positive\" half.\n\nThis is the abstract reflection positivity theorem. The proof uses the PD property of each φ_p and the product structure.\n\n**Key lemma:** If φ is PD on Gⁿ, then the \"one-plaquette reflection positivity\" holds: `∫ f·f∘θ·φ dμ ≥ 0` for f depending on the appropriate links.\n\nIf we can prove this for a SINGLE plaquette, then the product follows by induction (integrating out one plaquette at a time).\n\nBut as noted in §8.11.31 Finding 3, the single-plaquette case is NOT trivially ≥ 0. The PD property gives a double integral, not the single integral.\n\nHmm, let me think about this more carefully. For a SINGLE plaquette with four links (a, b, c, d), the reflection θ maps:\n- If the plaquette is entirely in the positive region: θ maps it to the negative region. The integral `∫ f·f∘θ·φ_p dμ` involves f at positive links and f∘θ at negative links. After integrating out the negative links (which only appear in f∘θ and the negative plaquette factors), we get... hmm, this is getting complicated.\n\nLet me think about the simplest case: a single plaquette that straddles the interface.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest approach might be to look at the problem from the perspective of the LITERATURE and find the exact proof structure. Let me search for the Osterwalder-Seiler proof.\n\nLet me search the literature.","turn":25,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":51,"role":"Tool","content":"---\nTitle: Heavy-Quark Masses from the Fermilab Method in Three-Flavor Lattice QCD\nAuthors: Elizabeth D. Freeland; Andreas S. Kronfeld; James N. Simone; Ruth S. Van de Water; Fermilab Lattice; MILC Collaborations\nAbstract: We report on heavy quark mass calculations using Fermilab heavy quarks. Lattice calculations of heavy-strange meson masses are combined with one-loop (automated) lattice perturbation theory to arrive at the quark mass. Mesons are constructed from Fermilab heavy quarks and staggered light quarks. We ...\n\n---\nTitle: Form factors for $B$ to $Kll$ semileptonic decay from three-flavor lattice QCD\nAuthors: Ran Zhou; Jon A. Bailey; Alexei Bazavov; Aida X. El-Khadra; Steven Gottlieb; Rajendra D. Jain; Andreas S. Kronfeld; Ruth S. Van de Water; Fermilab Lattice; MILC Collaborations\nAbstract: We study the $B \\to Kl^+l^-$ semileptonic decay process in three-flavor lattice QCD. We analyze several ensembles generated by the MILC collaboration at different lattice spacings and sea-quark masses. We use the asqtad improved staggered action for the light quarks and the clover action with the Fe...\n\n---\nTitle: Scalar lattice gauge theory\nAuthors: C. Wetterich\nAbstract: Scalar lattice gauge theories are models for scalar fields with local gauge symmetries. No fundamental gauge fields, or link variables in a lattice regularization, are introduced. The latter rather emerge as collective excitations composed from scalars. For suitable parameters scalar lattice gauge t...\n\n---\nTitle: Recent progress in lattice supersymmetry: from lattice gauge theory to black holes\nAuthors: Daisuke Kadoh\nAbstract: Supersymmetry (SUSY) is a fascinating topic in theoretical physics, because of its unique and counterintuitive properties. It is expected to emerge as new physics beyond the standard model, and it is also a building block for supergravity and superstring theory. A number of exact results obtained vi...\n\n---\nTitle: Matrix Product States for Lattice Field Theories\nAuthors: Mari Carmen Bañuls; Krzysztof Cichy; J. Ignacio Cirac; Karl Jansen; Hana Saito\nAbstract: The term Tensor Network States (TNS) refers to a number of families of states that represent different ansätze for the efficient description of the state of a quantum many-body system. Matrix Product States (MPS) are one particular case of TNS, and have become the most precise tool for the numerical...\n\n---\nTitle: Applications of Jarzynski's relation in lattice gauge theories\nAuthors: Alessandro Nada; Michele Caselle; Gianluca Costagliola; Marco Panero; Arianna Toniato\nAbstract: Jarzynski's equality is a well-known result in statistical mechanics, relating free-energy differences between equilibrium ensembles with fluctuations in the work performed during non-equilibrium transformations from one ensemble to the other. In this work, an extension of this relation to lattice g...\n\n---\nTitle: Recent results in large-N lattice gauge theories\nAuthors: Marco Panero\nAbstract: Generalizations of QCD in which the number of colors N is taken to infinity are characterized by profound mathematical properties, with far-reaching implications for fundamental problems and for phenomenological issues alike. In this contribution, after a brief introduction to the theoretical motiva...\n\n---\nTitle: The properties of $D1$-branes from lattice super Yang--Mills theory using gauge/gravity duality\nAuthors: Raghav Govind Jha\nAbstract: The two-dimensional supersymmetric Yang-Mills (SYM) theory with sixteen supercharges at large $N$ and strong 't~Hooft coupling is conjectured to be dual to certain supergravity solutions in the decoupling limit. We discretize the gauge theory preserving a subset of supersymmetries on the lattice. Ba...\n\nTime: 2026-08-05 08:13:13 UTC","timestamp":1785917593,"token_count":933,"tool_call_id":"7610fccd-c8a8-48c6-8c83-2737920fb3fe","full_token_estimate":778,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"search_literature","file_pat