{"id":53,"role":"Tool","content":"---\nTitle: Conditionally positive definite kernels in Hilbert $C^*$-modules\nAuthors: Mohammad Sal Moslehian\nAbstract: We investigate the notion of conditionally positive definite in the context of Hilbert $C^*$-modules and present a characterization of the conditionally positive definiteness in terms of the usual positive definiteness. We give a Kolmogorov type representation of conditionally positive definite kern...\n\n---\nTitle: Reflection Positivity---A Representation Theoretic Perspective\nAuthors: Karl-Hermann Neeb; Gestur Olafsson\nAbstract: Refection Positivity is a central theme at the crossroads of Lie group representations, euclidean and abstract harmonic analysis, constructive quantum field theory, and stochastic processes. This book provides the first presentation of the representation theoretic aspects of Refection Positivity and...\n\n---\nTitle: Handelman's Positivstellensatz for polynomial matrices positive definite on polyhedra\nAuthors: Công-Trình Lê; Thi-Hoa-Binh Du\nAbstract: In this paper we give a matrix version of Handelman's Positivstellensatz [1], representing polynomial matrices which are positive definite on convex, compact polyhedra. Moreover, we propose also a procedure to find such a representation. As a corollary of Handelman's theorem, we give a special case ...\n\n---\nTitle: Wilson lines and their Laurent positivity\nAuthors: Tsukasa Ishibashi; Hironori Oya\nAbstract: For a marked surface $Σ$ and a semisimple algebraic group $G$ of adjoint type, we study the Wilson line morphism $g_{[c]}:\\mathcal{P}_{G,Σ} \\to G$ associated with the homotopy class of an arc $c$ connecting boundary intervals of $Σ$, which is the comparison element of pinnings via parallel-transport...\n\n---\nTitle: Positive definite metric spaces\nAuthors: Mark W. Meckes\nAbstract: Magnitude is a numerical invariant of finite metric spaces, recently introduced by T. Leinster, which is analogous in precise senses to the cardinality of finite sets or the Euler characteristic of topological spaces. It has been extended to infinite metric spaces in several a priori distinct ways. ...\n\n---\nTitle: On the Riemann-Hilbert factorization problem for positive definite functions\nAuthors: Dan Kucerovsky; Amir T. P. Najafabadi; Aydin Sarraf\nAbstract: We give several general theorems concerning positive definite solutions of Riemann-Hilbert problems on the real line. Furthermore, as an example, we apply our theory to the characteristic function of a class of Lévy processes and we find the distribution of their extrema at a given stopping time....\n\n---\nTitle: On the transfer matrix of the supersymmetric eight-vertex model. I. Periodic boundary conditions\nAuthors: Christian Hagendorf; Jean Liénardy\nAbstract: The square-lattice eight-vertex model with vertex weights $a,b,c,d$ obeying the relation $(a^2+ab)(b^2+ab) = (c^2+ab)(d^2+ab)$ and periodic boundary conditions is considered. It is shown that the transfer matrix of the model for $L=2n+1$ vertical lines and periodic boundary conditions along the hori...\n\n---\nTitle: Characterization of Strict Positive Definiteness on products of complex spheres\nAuthors: Mario H. Castro; Eugenio Massa; Ana Paula Peron\nAbstract: In this paper we consider Positive Definite functions on products $Ω_{2q}\\timesΩ_{2p}$ of complex spheres, and we obtain a condition, in terms of the coefficients in their disc polynomial expansions, which is necessary and sufficient for the function to be Strictly Positive Definite. The result incl...\n\nTime: 2026-08-05 08:14:30 UTC","timestamp":1785917670,"token_count":876,"tool_call_id":"6410f1ba-0e3f-4de7-817e-55d25a56871d","full_token_estimate":732,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"search_literature","file_path":null,"old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":null,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":924,"edit_line":null},"turn":26,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":54,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1785917890,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean\",\"limit\":80,\"offset\":440}","name":"read_file"},"id":"b75e0e56-5667-464a-93a8-1f1e474ca011","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean\",\"pattern\":\"def wilsonActionOSInterface|def wilsonActionOSPositive|def wilsonActionOSNegative|def wilsonActionFinite\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"d9cdf8e5-480f-4fd7-8d1e-9dba5f2dc498","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":4164,"reasoning_content":"The literature search found \"Reflection Positivity—A Representation Theoretic Perspective\" by Neeb and Olafsson, which might be relevant. Let me also search for the specific Osterwalder-Seiler reference and the Lüscher approach.\n\nActually, let me take a step back and think about this more carefully. I've been going deep into the math but I should think about what's the most PRACTICAL path forward for the formalization.\n\nLet me reconsider the problem. The key identity is:\n`∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ`\n\nThis is proven (the integrand identity is in `G_thetaG_factorization_clean`). And `exp(-β·S(U)) = ∏_p φ_p(U)` is a product of PD plaquette factors.\n\nNow, the standard proof of reflection positivity for the Wilson action. Let me think about the cleanest version.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the approach from a completely different angle. Instead of the character expansion (which leads to the reorganization obstacle), let me think about the **direct PD kernel** approach.\n\n**Key idea:** The reflection positivity integral `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ` can be written as a PD quadratic form if we can show that `exp(-β·S(U))` with the reflection gives a PD kernel.\n\nSpecifically, define the \"reflection kernel\" `K(U, V) = exp(-β·S(merge(U, θV)))` where merge combines the positive links of U and the reflected negative links of V. Then `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ = ∫∫ f(U)·f(V)·K(U, V) dμ dμ`... no, that's not quite right.\n\nLet me think more carefully. The integral is over ALL links. f depends on pos+interface. f(θU) depends on neg+interface (after reflection). The Boltzmann factor exp(-β·S) depends on all links.\n\nAfter the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), the integral becomes:\n`∫∫ f(u⁺, u⁰)·f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·S(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nNow, `S(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = S⁺(u⁺) + S⁻(reflect(V⁺)) + S_int(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))`.\n`= S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_int(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))`.\n\nSo the integral is:\n`∫∫ f(u⁺, u⁰)·f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_int(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nThis is a double integral over (u⁺, u⁰) and (V⁺, σ(u⁰))... but u⁰ is shared. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me write it as:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰)·f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nNow, define `g(u⁺, u⁰) = f(u⁺, u⁰)·exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)/2)` and `h(V⁺, u⁰) = f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2)`.\n\nThen `I = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰)·h(V⁺, u⁰)·exp(-β·S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`.\n\nNow, `exp(-β·S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))` is the interface Boltzmann factor, which couples u⁺, u⁰, and reflect(V⁺). This is a product of PD plaquette factors.\n\nIf we can show that `exp(-β·S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))` is a PD kernel in (u⁺, u⁰) and (V⁺, u⁰), then `I = ∫∫ g(x)·h(y)·K(x, y) dx dy` would be ≥ 0 if g = h and K is PD.\n\nBut g ≠ h in general (g uses u⁰, h uses σ(u⁰)). And K is a kernel, not obviously PD.\n\nHmm, this is still complicated. Let me think about the Lüscher decomposition more carefully.\n\nActually, let me look at the Neeb-Olafsson book on reflection positivity, which might have the cleanest formulation.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. I think the key issue is that I need to understand the EXACT structure of the interface plaquettes and how the PD property applies. Let me look at the code for the interface action and plaquette structure.\n\nLet me look at the interface action decomposition and the plaquette types.\n\nActually, let me take a step back and think about what's the most practical thing to do in this session. I've spent a lot of time analyzing. Let me:\n\n1. Look at the existing code structure more carefully.\n2. Search for the Osterwalder-Seiler proof in the literature/web.\n3. Decide on a concrete approach and start implementing.\n\nLet me search the web for the Osterwalder-Seiler proof structure.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The project has been working on this for 27+ sessions. The character expansion approach is proven but the reorganization is stuck. The temporal expansion approach is promising but has issues.\n\nLet me think about whether there's a SIMPLER approach that I'm missing.\n\n**Key observation:** The `reflection_positivity_reorganization` lemma requires the Gram matrix form `d_w(x)·conj(d_w(y))`. The fullReflect form has `A_{w*}(σ(u⁰))·A_w(u⁰)` which is NOT of this form.\n\nBut what if we DON'T use the character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor, and instead use the character expansion of the TEST FUNCTION?\n\nThe test function f depends on pos+interface links. We can expand f in the Peter-Weyl basis of ALL pos+interface links:\n`f(U) = ∑_α c_α · Φ_α(U)` where Φ_α = ∏_l (ρ_{λ_l}(U(l)))_{i_l, j_l}.\n\nThen `f(θU) = ∑_α c_α · Φ_α(θU) = ∑_α c_α · Φ̃_α(U)` where Φ̃_α involves ρ(θU(l)) = ρ(U(θl))⁻¹ if temporal, ρ(U(θl)) if spatial.\n\nFor pos+interface links, θ maps pos→neg and interface→interface (with temporal inverted). So f(θU) depends on neg+interface links, which is a DIFFERENT set of links than f(U) (pos+interface).\n\nSo the expansion of f and f(θU) are in DIFFERENT bases (different links). This is the fundamental issue.\n\n**The resolution (§8.11.32):** Expand f ONLY in the temporal interface links w (which are inverted by θ), keeping the spatial interface links u⁰_s and positive links u⁺ as parameters. Then f(θU) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹), and the w-expansion of f(θU) uses the SAME w-basis (with w⁻¹ → conjugation).\n\nBut the issue is that the w-integral doesn't factorize simply (because w(x) appears in multiple plaquettes).\n\nHmm, but actually, if we expand BOTH the test function f AND the interface Boltzmann factor in the w-basis, then the w-integral becomes a product of triple product integrals. This is the character expansion approach, but restricted to temporal links.\n\nWait, that's essentially what the fullReflect form does! The fullReflect form expands the interface Boltzmann factor in characters over ALL interface links (including temporal). The issue is the reorganization.\n\nLet me think about this differently. What if we expand the interface Boltzmann factor in characters over ONLY the temporal interface links (not the spatial ones)?\n\n`exp(-β·S_int) = exp(-β·S_spatial_0) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower)`\n\nThe temporal links w appear only in S_ts_upper. So `exp(-β·S_ts_upper)` can be expanded in characters of w:\n`exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) = ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · χ_γ(w)`\n\nwhere χ_γ(w) = ∏_x χ_{γ_x}(w_x) is a character of the product group SU(N)^{spatial sites} and a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) are the coefficients (depending on u⁺ and u⁰_s through the other links in the plaquettes).\n\nThen the w-integral becomes:\n`∫ f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) dμ_w`\n`= ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w`\n\nNow, expand f in the w-basis:\n`f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = ∑_α c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_α(w)`\n`f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∑_β c_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_β(w⁻¹) = ∑_β c_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(Φ̃_β(w))`\n\nwhere Φ̃_β(w) is the conjugate basis (using ρ(w⁻¹) = ρ(w)†).\n\nThen:\n`∫ f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w`\n`= ∑_α ∑_β c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · c_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · ∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ̃_β(w)) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w`\n\nThe integral `∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ̃_β(w)) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w` is a PRODUCT of triple product integrals (one per spatial site x), each of the form `∫ (ρ_{λ_x}(w_x))_{i,j} · conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w_x))_{k,l}) · χ_{γ_x}(w_x) dμ(w_x)`.\n\nBy `triple_product_character_matrix_integral`, this equals `(1/dims μ_x) · ∑_a cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a, i, k) · conj(cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a, j, l))`.\n\nThis is a Gram matrix! So the w-integral gives:\n`∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_α ∑_β c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · c_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · [Gram matrix in α, β]`\n\nNow, the Gram matrix is `∑_a P_γ(α, a) · conj(P_γ(β, a))` where P_γ(α, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, i_x, k_x) (product of CG coefficients).\n\nSo the w-integral is:\n`∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a [∑_α c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(α, a)] · conj([∑_β c_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(β, a)])`\n`= ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) · conj(D_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a))`\n\nwhere `D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) = ∑_α c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(α, a)`.\n\nNow, the FULL integral (after the w-integral) is:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · [∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) · conj(D_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a))] dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰_s`\n\nWait, I need to be more careful. Let me redo this.\n\nThe full integral is:\n`I = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ`\n\nAfter the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺):\n`I = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰)·f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_int(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nNow, `S_int(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))` involves u⁺, u⁰, and reflect(V⁺). The temporal interface links w = u⁰_temporal appear in S_ts_upper (coupling u⁺ and w) and... do they appear in the part involving reflect(V⁺)?\n\nLet me think. The interface plaquettes are:\n- S_spatial_0: pure spatial at t=0, depends on u⁰_s only.\n- S_ts_upper: at t=0, mixed (0,i), involves w, u⁰_s, and positive spatial links at t=1 (part of u⁺).\n- S_ts_lower: at t=-1, mixed (0,i), involves negative temporal links at t=-1, u⁰_s, and negative spatial links at t=-1.\n\nAfter the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), the negative links become reflect(V⁺). So S_ts_lower becomes a function of reflect(V⁺) and u⁰_s. As I analyzed earlier, the reflected S_ts_lower involves positive temporal links at t=1 (from reflect(V⁺)) and u⁰_s. It does NOT involve w.\n\nSo `S_int(extend(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)`.\n\nAnd w appears ONLY in S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) and in f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) and f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) (through σ(u⁰) which inverts w).\n\nWait, σ(u⁰) inverts the temporal links w and keeps the spatial links u⁰_s. So f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) = f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹).\n\nSo the w-dependent part of the integrand is:\n`f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w))`\n\nAnd the w-independent part is:\n`exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)))`\n\nSo:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺)/2 + S⁺(V⁺)/2 + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))) · [∫_w f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) dμ_w] dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰_s`\n\nWait, I need to be more careful with the S⁺ factors. Let me redo:\n\n`exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)))`\n`= exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))`\n\nNow, the w-integral:\n`J(u⁺, V⁺, u⁰_s) = ∫_w f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) dμ_w`\n\nAfter expanding f and S_ts_upper in the w-basis:\n`J = ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) · conj(D_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a))`\n\nwhere a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) ≥ 0 (from the PD property of the plaquette factor) and D_γ = ∑_α c_α · P_γ(α, a).\n\nNow, the full integral:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) · conj(D_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a)) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰_s`\n\nNow, the key question: is this ≥ 0?\n\nIf a_γ were CONSTANT (independent of u⁺), then:\n`I = ∑_γ a_γ · ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∑_a |∫_{u⁺} exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)) · D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) dμ⁺|² · [something with S_ts_lower_reflected] dμ⁰_s`\n\nBut a_γ is NOT constant — it depends on u⁺ through the other links in the plaquettes. And S_ts_lower_reflected depends on V⁺, breaking the symmetry between u⁺ and V⁺.\n\nHmm, this is the issue. The S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) factor breaks the symmetry. If S_ts_lower_reflected were S_ts_upper(V⁺, u⁰_s, w) (same form as the u⁺ part), then we'd have symmetry and could get |...|². But S_ts_lower_reflected is a DIFFERENT function (involving positive temporal links at t=1, not w).\n\nWait, but actually, let me reconsider. The S_ts_lower_reflected involves positive temporal links at t=1 and u⁰_s. And S_ts_upper involves w (temporal interface links at t=0), u⁰_s, and positive spatial links at t=1. These are DIFFERENT links. So the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals are NOT symmetric.\n\nBUT — the a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) comes from the character expansion of exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) in w. The coefficients a_γ depend on u⁺ through the positive spatial links at t=1 (which are part of u⁺) and u⁰_s. And S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) depends on V⁺ through the positive temporal links at t=1 (which are part of V⁺) and u⁰_s.\n\nSo the u⁺ integral involves a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)), and the V⁺ integral involves conj(D_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)).\n\nThe u⁺ and V⁺ integrals are NOT the same because:\n1. u⁺ has a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) (from S_ts_upper), V⁺ has exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)).\n2. These are different functions of the positive links.\n\nSo the integral is NOT |...|², and it's NOT obviously ≥ 0.\n\nUNLESS... a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)) and exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) are related in a way that gives a PD kernel.\n\nActually, let me think about this. The key is that:\n`a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺))` is the γ-component of `exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)))` after expanding in w. This is the FULL positive Boltzmann factor (S⁺ + S_ts_upper), which is a product of PD plaquette factors (positive plaquettes + upper interface plaquettes). So `a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺))` is the γ-Fourier coefficient of this PD function in w.\n\nAnd `exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))` is `exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)))`. Is this the same as the FULL positive Boltzmann factor? \n\nS⁺(V⁺) is the positive action (positive plaquettes). S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) is the reflected lower interface action, involving positive temporal links at t=1 and u⁰_s. \n\nHmm, S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) is NOT the same as S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) because:\n- S⁺(u⁺) includes ALL positive plaquettes (at all positive times).\n- S_ts_upper involves w and positive spatial links at t=1.\n- S_ts_lower_reflected involves positive temporal links at t=1 and u⁰_s.\n\nSo S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) = S⁺(V⁺) + [reflected lower interface plaquettes]. And S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = S⁺(u⁺) + [upper interface plaquettes involving w].\n\nThese are DIFFERENT sets of plaquettes. The upper interface plaquettes involve w (temporal interface) and positive spatial links at t=1. The reflected lower interface plaquettes involve positive temporal links at t=1 and u⁰_s (spatial interface). So they involve DIFFERENT positive links (spatial at t=1 vs temporal at t=1).\n\nThis means the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals are genuinely different, and the integral is NOT a simple |...|².\n\nOK, I think the issue is that the interface plaquettes come in different types, and the temporal/spatial split doesn't cleanly separate them. Let me look at the actual plaquette structure in the code to understand this better.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe I'm overcomplicating this. Let me think about the Lüscher decomposition more carefully.\n\nThe Lüscher decomposition says: T = V^{1/2} · U · V^{1/2} where V is a positive multiplication operator and U is a positive integral operator. The key is that V and U are defined in terms of the plaquette factors.\n\nIn the transfer matrix formalism:\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺)/2 + S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)`\n\nThe Lüscher decomposition splits S_int into a part that depends on u⁺ (absorbed into V) and a part that doesn't (the U operator). But the interface plaquettes couple u⁺, u⁰, and V⁺, so the split is not clean.\n\nHmm, I think the issue is that the standard Lüscher decomposition works for a SPECIFIC form of the action (the Wilson action with a specific plaquette structure), and the formalization needs to match this exactly.\n\nLet me look at the actual plaquette structure in the code. Let me search for the interface plaquette types.\n\nActually, I think I should look at this from a higher level. Let me search the web for the Osterwalder-Seiler proof or the Lüscher transfer matrix construction.\n\nLet me search for \"Luscher transfer matrix reflection positivity plaquette\" or similar.\n\nActually, let me look at the existing code for the interface action decomposition. The design doc mentions `S_int = S_spatial_0 + S_ts_upper + S_ts_lower` but this is NOT in the code. Let me check if there's any decomposition of the interface action.\n\nLet me search for the interface action definition and any decomposition.","turn":28,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":55,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n-- 2618 lines, 153185 bytes --\n440 | /-- The sum over all oriented plaquettes (n; μ, ν) where all four corners have\n441 | negative signed time. This is the correct negative-time part of the\n442 | Osterwalder-Seiler decomposition. -/\n443 | noncomputable def wilsonActionOSNegative (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n444 |     (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n445 |   ∑ n : PeriodicSite T L, ∑ μ : Fin 4, ∑ ν : Fin 4,\n446 |     if signedTime T n.time < 0 ∧ signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L n μ).time < 0 ∧\n447 |        signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L (addVectorPeriodic T L n μ) ν).time < 0 ∧\n448 |        signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L n ν).time < 0 then\n449 |       plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n450 |     else 0\n451 | \n452 | /-- The sum over the remaining oriented plaquettes (those with corners on both sides\n453 | of the time interface). This is the interface part of the\n454 | Osterwalder-Seiler decomposition. -/\n455 | noncomputable def wilsonActionOSInterface (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\n456 |     (U : LinkVariable (SU N) (PeriodicSite T L)) : ℝ :=\n457 |   ∑ n : PeriodicSite T L, ∑ μ : Fin 4, ∑ ν : Fin 4,\n458 |     if ¬ (signedTime T n.time > 0 ∧ signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L n μ).time > 0 ∧\n459 |           signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L (addVectorPeriodic T L n μ) ν).time > 0 ∧\n460 |           signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L n ν).time > 0) ∧\n461 |        ¬ (signedTime T n.time < 0 ∧ signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L n μ).time < 0 ∧\n462 |           signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L (addVectorPeriodic T L n μ) ν).time < 0 ∧\n463 |           signedTime T (addVectorPeriodic T L n ν).time < 0) then\n464 |       plaquetteContribution N β U n μ ν\n465 |     else 0\n466 | \n467 | /--\n468 | For each oriented plaquette (n; μ, ν), exactly one of the three conditions holds:\n4