{"id":56,"role":"Tool","content":"Searched for \"def wilsonActionOSInterface|def wilsonActionOSPositive|def wilsonActionOSNegative|def wilsonActionFinite\" in C:\\github\\YangMills\\src\\lean\\YangMills\\Proofs\\ReflectionPositivity.lean\n3 match(es):\nReflectionPositivity.lean:431: noncomputable def wilsonActionOSPositive (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\nReflectionPositivity.lean:443: noncomputable def wilsonActionOSNegative (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\nReflectionPositivity.lean:455: noncomputable def wilsonActionOSInterface (N T L : ℕ) (β : ℝ) [NeZero T] [NeZero L]\nTime: 2026-08-05 08:18:10 UTC","timestamp":1785917890,"token_count":175,"tool_call_id":"d9cdf8e5-480f-4fd7-8d1e-9dba5f2dc498","full_token_estimate":139,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"grep","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean","old_text":"def wilsonActionOSInterface|def wilsonActionOSPositive|def wilsonActionOSNegative|def wilsonActionFinite","new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":3,"byte_count":null,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":2,"edit_line":null},"turn":28,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":57,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1785918013,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"num_results\":8,\"query\":\"Osterwalder Seiler reflection positivity lattice gauge theory proof transfer matrix positive operator plaquette\"}","name":"web_search"},"id":"0670ca65-28eb-4aa1-9769-8d9032d336cb","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":2207,"reasoning_content":"OK so the action decomposition is:\n- `wilsonActionOSPositive`: plaquettes with all corners at positive time.\n- `wilsonActionOSNegative`: plaquettes with all corners at negative time.\n- `wilsonActionOSInterface`: the rest (plaquettes straddling the interface).\n- `total_decomposition_os_periodic`: S = S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int.\n\nAnd `G_thetaG_factorization_clean` proves `G(U)·G(θU) = f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·(S⁺ + S⁻ + S_int))`.\n\nSo the identity `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)` is essentially proven (the integrand identity is there, just need to integrate).\n\nNow, the key question: how to show `∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S) ≥ 0`?\n\nLet me think about the cleanest approach. I've been going back and forth. Let me settle on a strategy.\n\n**Strategy: Plaquette-by-plaquette integration using PD property.**\n\nThe standard Osterwalder-Seiler proof. Let me think about the cleanest version.\n\nThe integral is `I = ∫ f(U)·f(θU)·∏_p φ_p(U) dμ` where `φ_p = exp(c·Re Tr(U_p))` is PD.\n\nThe plaquettes can be grouped:\n1. **Positive plaquettes** (all corners positive): depend only on positive links.\n2. **Negative plaquettes** (all corners negative): depend only on negative links.\n3. **Interface plaquettes** (straddling): depend on pos, neg, and interface links.\n\nThe key insight: we can integrate out the links in a specific order, using the PD property at each step.\n\n**Step 1: Integrate out the negative links.** The negative plaquette factors depend only on negative links. f(θU) depends on neg+interface links (after reflection). So the negative links appear in f(θU) and the negative plaquette factors.\n\nAfter the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), the negative links become V⁺ (positive). The negative plaquette factors become positive plaquette factors of V⁺. And f(θU) = f(reflect(U⁻), σ(u⁰)) = f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)).\n\nSo after the change of variables:\n`I = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰)·f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·[∏_{P⁺} φ_p(u⁺)]·[∏_{P⁺} φ_p(V⁺)]·[∏_{P⁰} φ_p(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))] dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nwhere `∏_{P⁺} φ_p(u⁺)` are the positive plaquette factors (depending on u⁺), `∏_{P⁺} φ_p(V⁺)` are the same factors for V⁺, and `∏_{P⁰} φ_p` are the interface plaquette factors (coupling u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)).\n\nNow, the interface plaquette factors are the hard part. They couple u⁺, u⁰, and reflect(V⁺).\n\n**Step 2: Expand the interface plaquette factors in characters.** The interface Boltzmann factor `exp(-β·S_int) = ∏_{P⁰} φ_p` can be expanded in Peter-Weyl characters. This is the `interface_kernel_character_expansion` lemma (PROVEN).\n\nBut this leads to the fullReflect form, which has the reorganization obstacle.\n\n**Alternative Step 2: Integrate out the temporal interface links w.** The temporal interface links w appear in:\n- f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w): the first f-factor.\n- f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹): the second f-factor (through σ(u⁰) which inverts w).\n- Some interface plaquette factors (the upper ones involving w).\n\nThe spatial interface links u⁰_s appear in:\n- f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w): the first f-factor.\n- f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹): the second f-factor (through σ(u⁰) which keeps u⁰_s).\n- Some interface plaquette factors (both upper and lower).\n\nIf we integrate out w first, we get a Gram matrix (via the triple product integral). But the remaining integral over u⁺, V⁺, u⁰_s is not obviously ≥ 0 (as I analyzed above).\n\nHmm. Let me think about this differently.\n\n**Key insight:** The problem is that the interface plaquettes couple u⁺, V⁺, and u⁰ in a complex way. The standard proof handles this by the Lüscher decomposition, which requires a specific structure of the plaquette factors.\n\nLet me look at the Lüscher decomposition more carefully. The Lüscher transfer matrix is:\n`T = V^{1/2} · U · V^{1/2}`\n\nwhere:\n- V is a multiplication operator: `(Vg)(u) = v(u) · g(u)` where v(u) = exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)/2) · [spatial plaquette factor at t=0].\n- U is an integral operator: `(Ug)(u⁺, u⁰_s) = ∫ K(u⁺, V⁺, u⁰_s) · g(V⁺, u⁰_s) dμ⁺(V⁺)` where K is the temporal plaquette kernel.\n\nThe key is that V is a POSITIVE multiplication operator (v ≥ 0) and U is a POSITIVE integral operator (K is PD). Then T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} is positive.\n\nBut this requires:\n1. The interface action decomposes as S_int = S_spatial_0 + S_ts where S_spatial_0 depends only on u⁰_s (spatial interface) and S_ts depends on u⁺, V⁺, u⁰_s, w (temporal).\n2. The spatial part gives V (positive multiplication).\n3. The temporal part gives U (PD integral operator).\n\nThe issue is that S_ts couples u⁺, V⁺, u⁰_s, AND w. The w-dependence is the temporal interface link. After expanding in w and integrating out w, the temporal part becomes a PD kernel in (u⁺, u⁰_s) and (V⁺, u⁰_s).\n\nBut as I analyzed, the u⁺ and V⁺ parts are NOT symmetric because the upper and lower interface plaquettes involve different links.\n\nWAIT. Let me reconsider. Maybe I was wrong about the asymmetry. Let me think again.\n\nAfter the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), the interface plaquettes are:\n- S_spatial_0: pure spatial at t=0, depends on u⁰_s only. (Both u⁺ and V⁺ independent.)\n- S_ts_upper: at t=0, mixed (0,i), involves w, u⁰_s, and positive spatial links at t=1 (part of u⁺).\n- S_ts_lower (reflected): at t=-1 reflected to t=1, involves positive temporal links at t=1 (part of V⁺) and u⁰_s.\n\nHmm wait, but actually, the reflected S_ts_lower involves positive temporal links at t=1. And S_ts_upper involves positive spatial links at t=1. So the u⁺ integral involves S_ts_upper (with positive spatial links at t=1) and the V⁺ integral involves S_ts_lower_reflected (with positive temporal links at t=1).\n\nBut the POSITIVE action S⁺(u⁺) includes ALL positive plaquettes, including those at t=1 with mixed directions. So S⁺(u⁺) includes plaquettes at t=1 involving temporal links at t=1 AND spatial links at t=1. So S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) involves ALL plaquettes that touch u⁺ (positive links), including the upper interface plaquettes.\n\nSimilarly, S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) involves ALL plaquettes that touch V⁺ (positive links), including the reflected lower interface plaquettes.\n\nNow, the KEY question: are S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) and S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) the SAME function (with u⁺ ↔ V⁺ and w ↔ something)?\n\nS⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = [all positive plaquettes of u⁺] + [upper interface plaquettes involving w, u⁰_s, and positive spatial links at t=1].\n\nS⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) = [all positive plaquettes of V⁺] + [reflected lower interface plaquettes involving positive temporal links at t=1 and u⁰_s].\n\nThese are NOT the same because:\n- S_ts_upper involves w (temporal interface) and positive spatial links at t=1.\n- S_ts_lower_reflected involves positive temporal links at t=1 and u⁰_s (spatial interface).\n\nThe upper and lower interface plaquettes involve DIFFERENT types of positive links (spatial at t=1 vs temporal at t=1) and DIFFERENT types of interface links (w temporal vs u⁰_s spatial).\n\nSo the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals are genuinely asymmetric. This means the Lüscher decomposition (as I described it) does NOT directly apply.\n\nHmm, but the Lüscher decomposition IS supposed to work for the Wilson action. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that the Lüscher decomposition works for a SPECIFIC choice of \"time\" direction and a SPECIFIC decomposition of the plaquettes. The key is that the temporal links are the \"dynamical\" variables and the spatial links are the \"external\" variables.\n\nIn the standard Lüscher decomposition:\n- The transfer matrix evolves in the TIME direction.\n- The \"spatial\" links at each time slice are the state variables.\n- The temporal links connect adjacent time slices.\n\nThe transfer matrix at the interface integrates out the temporal interface link w, giving a kernel in the spatial links.\n\nBut in our case, the interface plaquettes involve BOTH temporal and spatial interface links, and the decomposition is more complex.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that I need to think about the transfer matrix differently. Instead of integrating out w, maybe I should think about the transfer matrix as evolving in the time direction, with the spatial links as the state.\n\nActually, I think the standard Lüscher transfer matrix for the Wilson action works as follows:\n\nThe transfer matrix T_n evolves from time slice n to time slice n+1. It integrates out the temporal links at time n (connecting slice n to slice n+1). The state is the spatial links at each slice.\n\nAt the interface (time 0), the transfer matrix T_0 integrates out the temporal interface links w (connecting time 0 to time 1). The state is the spatial links at time 0 (u⁰_s) and time 1.\n\nBut the reflection positivity involves the reflection θ, which inverts the temporal links. So the transfer matrix at the interface is T_0, and the reflection gives T_0† = T_0 (if T_0 is self-adjoint) or something related.\n\nThe key property: T_0 = B*·B where B is the \"half-transfer matrix\" that integrates out the temporal links. This gives T_0 ≥ 0.\n\nBut the formalization of this requires:\n1. The transfer matrix T_0 integrates out the temporal interface links w.\n2. The kernel of T_0 is a PD function of the spatial links.\n3. T_0 = B*·B.\n\nThe PD property of the kernel comes from the PD property of the plaquette factors.\n\nOK, I think I need to look at the actual Osterwalder-Seiler proof more carefully. Let me search the web for it.\n\nActually, let me look at the Neeb-Olafsson book on reflection positivity, which was found in the literature search. Let me fetch it.\n\nActually, let me search more specifically for the proof structure.","turn":29,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":58,"role":"Tool","content":"Search results (8):\n\n1. [https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-113/issue-3/General-proof-of-Osterwalder-Schrader-positivity-for-the-Wilson-action/cmp/1104160284.pdf]\n   PDF General Proof of Osterwalder-Schrader Positivity for the Wilson Action\n   Luscher [3] and independently Osterwalder and Seiler [4] have addressed the problem in two different ways. Luscher starting from the Wilson action builds up a Hubert space as a Fock space derived from equal time fields and explicitly constructs a transfer matrix [5] which he proves to be positive definite, in this context Osterwalder-Schrader positivity is proved for gauge invariant operators ...\n\n2. [https://ai.vixra.org/pdf/2607.0073v1.pdf]\n   PDF A Machine-Checked Reflection-Positivity Framework for ZN Lattice Gauge ...\n   1 What is proved, and what is not Reflection positivity is the hypothesis from which Osterwalder and Seiler [1] construct the phys-ical Hilbert space of a lattice gauge theory and its transfer operator. It is the first of four steps between a Euclidean lattice measure and a statement about a spectrum, the others being the Gelfand-Naimark-Segal quotient, the transfer operator itself, and ...\n\n3. [https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.19362]\n   [2606.19362] Reflection-Positive Construction of a Four-Dimensional SU ...\n   In the Euclidean view one must first require that positivity not be violated, and from this modest demand, together with locality, a great deal follows: starting from a reflection-positive lattice formulation of pure SU(N) Yang-Mills theory we obtain a transfer operator with a uniform gap, while large Wilson loops already show an area law by means of convergent character (polymer) expansions ...\n\n4. [https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0003491678900398]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - ScienceDirect\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories.\n\n5. [https://ai.vixra.org/pdf/2607.0078v1.pdf]\n   PDF From the Gibbs Weight to the Spectral Gap: A Complete Machine-Checked ...\n   Abstract A mass gap is a statement about the spectrum of an operator, but a lattice gauge theory is given as a measure. The passage between the two — the Osterwalder-Seiler reconstruction — proceeds through reflection positivity, a Gelfand-Naimark-Segal space, a transfer operator, and an identification theorem asserting that expectations in the measure are matrix elements of that ...\n\n6. [https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Gauge-field-theories-on-a-lattice-Osterwalder-Seiler/610728f713f24d54488c5e5be85d5d96092211e3]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - Semantic Scholar\n   Abstract We begin a rigorous, nonperturbative investigation of quantum field theories with local internal symmetries. We discuss the lattice approximation of Yang-Mills fields and of fermion fields in the Euclidean setup and we verify physical positivity for the Schwinger functions of these approximations. This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove ...\n\n7. [https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0003491678900398]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - ScienceDirect\n   This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence and analyticity of the infinite volume limit of strongly coupled Yang-Mills theories on the lattice and we verify Wilson's confinement bound. Finally we present a rigorous treatment of the Higgs mechanism in lattice gauge theories.\n\n8. [https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1978AnPhy.110..440O/abstract]\n   Gauge field theories on a lattice - NASA/ADS\n   We begin a rigorous, nonperturbative investigation of quantum field theories with local internal symmetries. We discuss the lattice approximation of Yang-Mills fields and of fermion fields in the Euclidean setup and we verify physical positivity for the Schwinger functions of these approximations. This implies the existence of a positive self-adjoint transfer matrix. We then prove existence ...\n\n\nTime: 2026-08-05 08:20:14 UTC","timestamp":1785918014,"token_count":1074,"tool_call_id":"0670ca65-28eb-4aa1-9769-8d9032d336cb","full_token_estimate":896,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"web_search","file_path":null,"old_text":"Osterwalder Seiler reflection positivity lattice gauge theory proof transfer matrix positive operator plaquette","new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":null,"byte_count":4286,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":1077,"edit_line":null},"turn":29,"is_prune_marker":false}
