{"id":61,"role":"Assistant","content":"The PDF isn't text-extractable. Let me check the vixra papers which appear related to this project, and the arxiv paper on reflection-positive SU(N) construction.\nTime: 2026-08-05 08:25:51 UTC","timestamp":1785918351,"token_count":45,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://ai.vixra.org/pdf/2607.0078v1.pdf\",\"max_bytes\":50000}","name":"fetch_url"},"id":"f18cdf97-0c00-4c72-aa11-24fcb29e4e16","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":4010,"reasoning_content":"The PDF is not readable in text form. Let me try the vixra papers which seem to be from this very project. Let me check the vixra paper about the machine-checked reflection positivity framework.\n\nActually, let me look at the vixra papers - they seem to be related to this project. Let me fetch the one about \"From the Gibbs Weight to the Spectral Gap\".\n\nActually, wait - these vixra papers might BE from this project. Let me check.\n\nLet me instead focus on the arxiv paper 2606.19362 which is about \"Reflection-Positive Construction of a Four-Dimensional SU...\" - this might have the proof structure.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what I know and what the cleanest path forward is.\n\nI've spent a lot of time analyzing the math. Let me consolidate what I know:\n\n1. The identity `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)` is proven (integrand identity in `G_thetaG_factorization_clean`).\n\n2. `exp(-β·S) = ∏_p φ_p` is a product of PD plaquette factors.\n\n3. The fullReflect form (character expansion of interface Boltzmann) is proven but the reorganization is stuck.\n\n4. The temporal expansion approach (§8.11.32) is promising but has the a_γ U⁺-dependence issue.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest path. I think the key insight I've been missing is:\n\n**The full integral `∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S) dμ` can be written as a DOUBLE integral that IS a PD quadratic form.**\n\nHere's the key: after the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), the integral becomes:\n`I = ∫∫ f(u⁺, u⁰)·f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nNow, define `g(u⁺, u⁰) = f(u⁺, u⁰)·exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)/2)` and note that `f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2)` is the \"conjugate\" factor.\n\nThen `I = ∫∫ g(u⁺, u⁰)·h(V⁺, u⁰)·exp(-β·S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)`\n\nwhere `h(V⁺, u⁰) = f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))·exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2)`.\n\nNow, the KEY question: is `exp(-β·S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))` a PD kernel in (u⁺, u⁰) and (V⁺, u⁰)?\n\nIf it is, then `I = ∫∫ g(x)·h(y)·K(x, y) dx dy` and if g = h (which requires f(u⁺, u⁰) = f(V⁺, σ(u⁰))... no, that's not right).\n\nActually, the PD quadratic form is `∫∫ g(x)·conj(g(y))·K(x, y) dx dy ≥ 0`. But here we have `g(u⁺, u⁰)·h(V⁺, u⁰)` where h ≠ conj(g) in general (because h uses σ(u⁰) while g uses u⁰).\n\nHmm, so it's NOT a standard PD quadratic form because of the σ twist on u⁰.\n\nBUT - the σ twist only affects the TEMPORAL interface links (inverting them). The spatial interface links are unchanged. So if we expand in the temporal links, the σ twist becomes a conjugation (ρ(w⁻¹) = ρ(w)†), and the PD structure emerges.\n\nThis is exactly the §8.11.32 approach! The σ twist on temporal links provides the conjugation needed for the PD quadratic form.\n\nOK so let me think about this more carefully. The integral is:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_int(u⁺, u⁰_s, w, reflect(V⁺))) dμ⁺ dμ⁺ dμ⁰_s dμ_w`\n\nwhere `g(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)·exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)/2)` and `h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)·exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2)`.\n\nNow, `S_int(u⁺, u⁰_s, w, reflect(V⁺)) = S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)`.\n\nThe w-integral:\n`J(u⁺, V⁺, u⁰_s) = ∫_w g(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) dμ_w`\n\nNow, expand g and h in the w-basis:\n`g(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = ∑_α c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_α(w)`\n`h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∑_β d_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_β(w⁻¹) = ∑_β d_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(Φ̃_β(w))`\n\nwhere Φ̃_β(w) is the conjugate basis (using ρ(w⁻¹) = ρ(w)†, so (ρ_λ(w⁻¹))_{ij} = conj((ρ_λ(w))_{ji})).\n\nAnd expand `exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w))` in characters of w:\n`exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) = ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · χ_γ(w)`\n\nwhere a_γ ≥ 0 (PD property of the plaquette factor).\n\nThen:\n`J = ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_α ∑_β c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · d_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · ∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ̃_β(w)) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w`\n\nThe integral `∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ̃_β(w)) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w` is a product of triple product integrals, giving a Gram matrix:\n`= ∑_a P_γ(α, a) · conj(P_γ(β, a))` (up to normalization factors).\n\nSo:\n`J = ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a [∑_α c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(α, a)] · conj([∑_β d_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(β, a)])`\n`= ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) · conj(E_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a))`\n\nwhere `D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) = ∑_α c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(α, a)` and `E_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a) = ∑_β d_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(β, a)`.\n\nNow, the full integral:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · ∑_a D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) · conj(E_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a)) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁰_s`\n\nNow, the key: `a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s)` comes from the character expansion of `exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w))` in w. This means:\n`exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) = ∑_γ a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · χ_γ(w)`\n\nAnd `a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺))` is the γ-Fourier coefficient of `exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)))` in w. This is the FULL positive Boltzmann factor (positive plaquettes + upper interface plaquettes), which is a product of PD plaquette factors.\n\nSimilarly, `exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺))` is `exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)))`. Is this also a product of PD plaquette factors?\n\nS⁺(V⁺) is the positive action (all positive plaquettes). S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) is the reflected lower interface action. Together, S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s) is the action on all plaquettes that touch V⁺ (positive links), including the reflected lower interface plaquettes.\n\nBut wait - S⁺(V⁺) already includes ALL positive plaquettes. And S_ts_lower_reflected involves positive temporal links at t=1 and u⁰_s. Are the reflected lower interface plaquettes already counted in S⁺(V⁺)?\n\nNo! The reflected lower interface plaquettes involve u⁰_s (spatial interface links), which are NOT part of V⁺ (positive links). So S_ts_lower_reflected is NOT part of S⁺(V⁺). It's a separate set of plaquettes involving V⁺ and u⁰_s.\n\nSo `exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)))` is a product of:\n- Positive plaquette factors (from S⁺(V⁺)): PD on positive links.\n- Reflected lower interface plaquette factors (from S_ts_lower_reflected): PD on (V⁺ temporal links at t=1, u⁰_s).\n\nSo the full thing is a product of PD factors, hence PD.\n\nNow, the integral becomes:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∑_γ ∑_a [∫_{u⁺} a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)) · D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a) dμ⁺] · conj([∫_{V⁺} exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) · E_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a) dμ⁺]) dμ⁰_s`\n\nWait, but this requires the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals to be conjugates of each other. They're NOT conjugates because:\n- u⁺ integral: `a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)) · D_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s, a)`\n- V⁺ integral: `exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) · E_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s, a)`\n\nThese are different because:\n1. u⁺ has `a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s)` (from S_ts_upper), V⁺ has `exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))` (from S_ts_lower).\n2. D_γ and E_γ are different (D from c_α, E from d_β).\n\nSo the integral is NOT |...|². It's a product of two DIFFERENT integrals.\n\nBUT - if D_γ = E_γ (i.e., c_α = d_α, meaning the Peter-Weyl expansion of g and h are the same), AND a_γ(u⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)) = exp(-β·S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)) (with u⁺ ↔ V⁺), then it would be |...|².\n\nAre c_α = d_α? Well, g(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)·exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)/2) and h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)·exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2). The Peter-Weyl expansion of g in w gives c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s), and the expansion of h in w⁻¹ gives d_β(V⁺, u⁰_s). Since h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)·exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2), and f is real, we have h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)·exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2).\n\nNow, the expansion of h in w⁻¹: `h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∑_β d_β(V⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_β(w⁻¹)`. And `Φ_β(w⁻¹) = ∏_x (ρ_{λ_x}(w_x⁻¹))_{i_x, j_x} = ∏_x conj((ρ_{λ_x}(w_x))_{j_x, i_x})`.\n\nSo if we define `Φ̃_β(w) = ∏_x (ρ_{λ_x}(w_x))_{j_x, i_x}` (with indices swapped), then `Φ_β(w⁻¹) = conj(Φ̃_β(w))`.\n\nAnd the expansion of g in w: `g(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = ∑_α c_α(u⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_α(w)`.\n\nFor the Gram matrix to work, we need the SAME basis functions in both expansions. But g is expanded in Φ_α(w) and h is expanded in conj(Φ̃_β(w)). These are the SAME basis if Φ̃_β = Φ_β, which requires (ρ_λ(w))_{j,i} = (ρ_λ(w))_{i,j}, i.e., the representation matrices are symmetric. This is NOT true in general.\n\nHmm, so the basis functions are different. But the triple product integral handles this: `∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ̃_β(w)) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w` is a triple product integral with the \"conjugate\" basis having swapped indices. The triple product integral lemma handles arbitrary matrix elements, so this is fine.\n\nThe issue is whether the result is a Gram matrix. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\n`∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ̃_β(w)) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w = ∏_x ∫ (ρ_{λ_x}(w_x))_{i_x, j_x} · conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w_x))_{l_x, k_x}) · χ_{γ_x}(w_x) dμ(w_x)`\n\nBy the triple product integral:\n`= ∏_x (1/dims μ_x) · ∑_a cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a, i_x, k_x) · conj(cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a, j_x, l_x))`\n\nThis IS a Gram matrix in (a, i_x, k_x) vs (a, j_x, l_x). So:\n`∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ̃_β(w)) · χ_γ(w) dμ_w = ∑_a P_γ(α, a) · conj(Q_γ(β, a))`\n\nwhere P_γ(α, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, i_x, k_x) and Q_γ(β, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, j_x, l_x).\n\nWait, but P and Q are DIFFERENT functions (P uses i_x, k_x and Q uses j_x, l_x). So this is NOT a standard Gram matrix `P(α)·conj(P(β))`. It's `P(α)·conj(Q(β))` where P ≠ Q.\n\nHmm, but actually, P and Q are related: P_γ(α, a) uses the \"row\" indices (i_x, k_x) of α and β, while Q_γ(β, a) uses the \"column\" indices (j_x, l_x). So if we think of α = (λ, i, j) and β = (μ, k, l), then:\n- P_γ(α, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, i_x, k_x) — uses i_x from α and k_x from β.\n- Q_γ(β, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, j_x, l_x) — uses j_x from α and l_x from β.\n\nWait, this is confusing. Let me be more careful.\n\nα = (λ_x, i_x, j_x)_x and β = (μ_x, k_x, l_x)_x.\n\nThe triple product at site x is:\n`∫ (ρ_{λ_x}(w_x))_{i_x, j_x} · conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w_x))_{l_x, k_x}) · χ_{γ_x}(w_x) dμ(w_x)`\n\nNote: the conj part is `conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w_x))_{l_x, k_x})` (indices l_x, k_x from β, but SWAPPED: the first index is l_x and the second is k_x).\n\nBy the triple product integral lemma:\n`∫ χ_{γ_x}(w) · (ρ_{λ_x}(w))_{i_x, j_x} · conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w))_{l_x, k_x}) dμ(w) = (1/dims μ_x) · ∑_a cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a, i_x, l_x) · conj(cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a, j_x, k_x))`\n\nSo the Gram matrix is in (a, i_x, l_x) vs (a, j_x, k_x). This means:\n- P_γ(α, β, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, i_x, l_x) — uses i_x from α and l_x from β.\n- Q_γ(α, β, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, j_x, k_x) — uses j_x from α and k_x from β.\n\nAnd the integral is `∑_a P_γ(α, β, a) · conj(Q_γ(α, β, a))`.\n\nThis is `∑_a P(a) · conj(Q(a))` which is `⟨P, Q⟩` (inner product), NOT `‖P‖²` (norm squared). So it's NOT necessarily ≥ 0 for a single γ.\n\nBUT - the sum over γ with a_γ ≥ 0 gives:\n`J = ∑_γ a_γ · ∑_α ∑_β c_α · d_β · ∑_a P_γ(α, β, a) · conj(Q_γ(α, β, a))`\n\nThis is `∑_γ a_γ · ⟨P_γ, Q_γ⟩` where P_γ and Q_γ are vectors depending on c and d. This is NOT necessarily ≥ 0.\n\nHmm, so even with the temporal expansion, the w-integral is NOT necessarily ≥ 0 for a single set of parameters. The non-negativity comes from the FULL integral (including the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals).\n\nOK, I think the fundamental issue is that the non-negativity is a JOINT property of all the integrals, and it cannot be shown by integrating out one variable at a time. The standard proof must use a different structure.\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the key is that the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals, combined with the w-integral, give a PD quadratic form.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The full integral is:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · h(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))) dμ⁺ dμ⁺ dμ⁰_s dμ_w`\n\nNow, the key insight: `S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)` is the action on all plaquettes involving u⁺ (positive links) and the upper interface. And `S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)` is the action on all plaquettes involving V⁺ (positive links) and the reflected lower interface.\n\nNow, the upper interface plaquettes involve w (temporal interface), u⁰_s (spatial interface), and positive SPATIAL links at t=1. The reflected lower interface plaquettes involve positive TEMPORAL links at t=1 and u⁰_s (spatial interface).\n\nSo the upper and lower interface plaquettes involve DIFFERENT positive links (spatial at t=1 vs temporal at t=1). This means the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals are genuinely asymmetric.\n\nBUT - what if we think of the FULL positive Boltzmann factor as a PD kernel? The full positive Boltzmann factor is:\n`B(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)))`\n\nThis is a product of PD plaquette factors (positive plaquettes + upper interface plaquettes). So B is PD as a function of all the links it depends on (u⁺, u⁰_s, w).\n\nSimilarly, the reflected lower Boltzmann factor is:\n`B'(V⁺, u⁰_s) = exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s)))`\n\nThis is also a product of PD plaquette factors. So B' is PD.\n\nNow, the integral is:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_w ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · B(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) dμ⁺ · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · B'(V⁺, u⁰_s) dμ⁺ dμ_w dμ⁰_s`\n\nWait, this isn't right either. Let me be more careful.\n\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_w [∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w))) dμ⁺] · [∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))) dμ⁺] dμ_w dμ⁰_s`\n\nWait, is this right? The w-integral is over w, and the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals are separate. But w appears in the u⁺ integral (through S_ts_upper) and in the V⁺ integral (through f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹)). And S_ts_lower_reflected does NOT depend on w.\n\nSo:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_w A(u⁰_s, w) · B(u⁰_s, w) dμ_w dμ⁰_s`\n\nwhere:\n`A(u⁰_s, w) = ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w))) dμ⁺`\n`B(u⁰_s, w) = ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))) dμ⁺`\n\nNow, `B(u⁰_s, w) = ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))) dμ⁺`.\n\nSince w → w⁻¹ is measure-preserving, `∫_w A(u⁰_s, w) · B(u⁰_s, w) dμ_w = ∫_w A(u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · B(u⁰_s, w⁻¹) dμ_w`.\n\nAnd `B(u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))) dμ⁺`.\n\nSo `I = ∫_{u⁰_s} exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∫_w A(u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · B'(u⁰_s, w) dμ_w dμ⁰_s`\n\nwhere `B'(u⁰_s, w) = ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, u⁰_s, w) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺) + S_ts_lower_reflected(V⁺, u⁰_s))) dμ⁺`.\n\nHmm, this doesn't simplify things. The issue is that A and B' are different functions (A involves S_ts_upper, B' involves S_ts_lower_reflected).\n\nOK, I think I've been going in circles. Let me take a completely different approach. Let me look at the actual Osterwalder-Seiler proof from a reliable source.\n\nLet me try fetching the arxiv paper 2606.19362 which is about reflection-positive construction of SU(N) Yang-Mills.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The vixra papers (2607.0073v1 and 2607.0078v1) seem to be from THIS project. Let me check them - they might contain the proof strategy that was already developed.\n\nLet me fetch the vixra paper about the machine-checked reflection positivity framework.","turn":32,"is_prune_marker":false}
