lection positivity for the FULL Wilson action using a plaquette-by-plaquette argument.**\n\nThe plaquette-by-plaquette argument would:\n1. Write `exp(-β·S) = ∏_p φ_p` (product of PD plaquette factors).\n2. Use the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺) to get a double integral.\n3. Show the interface Boltzmann factor is a PD kernel in (u⁺, u⁰) and (V⁺, u⁰).\n4. The PD property gives `∫∫ g(x)·conj(g(y))·K(x,y) ≥ 0`.\n\nBut the σ twist on u⁰ breaks the simple PD structure. The temporal expansion handles this.\n\nActually, let me think about this ONE MORE TIME. The key issue is the σ twist. What if we DON'T do the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), and instead work directly with the single integral `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S(U)) dμ`?\n\nThe single integral is over ALL links. f(U) depends on pos+interface, f(θU) depends on neg+interface (after reflection). The Boltzmann factor exp(-β·S) depends on all links.\n\nThe positive plaquette factors depend on pos+interface links. The negative plaquette factors depend on neg+interface links. The interface plaquette factors depend on pos+neg+interface links.\n\nNow, integrate out the negative links. The negative links appear in:\n- f(θU) (through the reflection, which maps neg → pos).\n- Negative plaquette factors.\n- Interface plaquette factors (lower part).\n\nAfter integrating out the negative links, we get a function of pos+interface links. By the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺), this becomes a function of V⁺ (positive) and u⁰ (interface).\n\nThis is the same as before. The σ twist is inherent in the reflection.\n\nOK, I think the key insight is:\n\n**The σ twist on temporal interface links is EXACTLY what provides the conjugation needed for the PD quadratic form.** Without the σ twist (i.e., if σ were the identity), the integral would be `∫ f·f·exp(-β·S) = ∫ f²·exp(-β·S) ≥ 0` trivially (since f² ≥ 0 and exp > 0). The σ twist makes it nontrivial, but the PD property of the plaquette factors ensures it's still ≥ 0.\n\nThe temporal expansion converts the σ twist into a conjugation (ρ(w⁻¹) = ρ(w)†), which then gives a Gram matrix structure.\n\nOK, I think I have a clear enough understanding. Let me document my analysis and prepare a handoff.\n\nLet me write a new section in the design doc documenting this session's analysis, and then prepare the handoff.\n\nActually, let me first check: is there a lemma that integrates `G_thetaG_factorization_clean` to get `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)`? Let me look at `G_thetaG_factorization` (line 3044) which seems to be a different version.\n\nLet me read that.","turn":34,"is_prune_marker":false}
