Q(δ, a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, j_x, k_x) — uses j from α and k from β.\n\nAnd the w-integral is `∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(Q(δ, a))`.\n\nNow, define δ' = ((λ, j, i), (μ, l, k)) (swap row/column indices). Then P(δ', a) = ∏_x cgME(γ_x, λ_x, μ_x, a_x, j_x, k_x) = Q(δ, a).\n\nSo the w-integral is `∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a))`.\n\nNow, the full expression (summing over δ = (α, β)):\n`∑_δ c_δ(u⁺, V⁺) · ∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a))`\n\nwhere c_δ(u⁺, V⁺) = c_α(u⁺) · c_β(V⁺).\n\nThis is `∑_δ c_δ · ∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a))`. If δ' = δ (i.e., the index swap is the identity), this would be `∑_δ c_δ · ‖P(δ)‖² ≥ 0` (if c_δ ≥ 0). But δ' ≠ δ in general.\n\nHowever, if we sum over ALL δ, then δ' ranges over the same set (the index swap is a bijection). So:\n`∑_δ c_δ · ∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a)) = ∑_δ c_{δ'} · ∑_a P(δ', a) · conj(P(δ, a))` (reindexing δ ↔ δ')\n\nTaking the average:\n`= (1/2) ∑_δ (c_δ · ∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a)) + c_{δ'} · ∑_a P(δ', a) · conj(P(δ, a)))`\n`= (1/2) ∑_δ ∑_a (c_δ · P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a)) + c_{δ'} · P(δ', a) · conj(P(δ, a)))`\n`= (1/2) ∑_δ ∑_a (c_δ · P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a)) + conj(c_δ · P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a))))` (if c is real)\n`= ∑_δ ∑_a Re(c_δ · P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a)))`\n\nHmm, this is the real part of `∑_δ c_δ · ∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a))`. But this is NOT necessarily ≥ 0.\n\nWait, but f is REAL. So c_α are the Peter-Weyl coefficients of a real function. For a real function, the coefficients satisfy `c_{dual(λ), j, i} = conj(c_{λ, i, j})` (reality constraint). So c_δ and c_{δ'} are related by conjugation.\n\nSpecifically, if δ = ((λ, i, j), (μ, k, l)) and δ' = ((λ, j, i), (μ, l, k)), then:\nc_δ = c_{λ, i, j}(u⁺) · c_{μ, k, l}(V⁺)\nc_{δ'} = c_{λ, j, i}(u⁺) · c_{μ, l, k}(V⁺) = conj(c_{λ, i, j}(u⁺)) · conj(c_{μ, k, l}(V⁺)) = conj(c_δ)\n\n(Since f is real, c_{λ, j, i} = conj(c_{λ, i, j}).)\n\nSo c_{δ'} = conj(c_δ). Then:\n`∑_δ c_δ · ∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a)) = ∑_δ c_δ · ∑_a P(δ, a) · conj(P(δ', a))`\n\nAnd using the reindexing:\n`= ∑_