([∫_{V⁺} exp(-β·(S⁺ + S_ts_lower_reflected)) · c_β · Q_γ(α, β, a) dμ⁺]) dμ⁰_s`\n\nWait, but P_γ(α, β, a) depends on β (through μ, l), so the u⁺ integral depends on β. And Q_γ(α, β, a) depends on α (through λ, j), so the V⁺ integral depends on α. So the u⁺ and V⁺ integrals are coupled through the α, β sums.\n\nThis means we CANNOT write I as `∑ |∫ ...|²`. The expression is more complex.\n\nOK, I think the fundamental issue is that the triple product integral gives a Gram matrix that COUPLES the α and β indices, preventing the factorization into |...|².\n\nThis is a deep mathematical challenge. The standard proof must use a different structure to handle this.\n\nLet me think about what the standard proof does. I believe the key is the Lüscher decomposition, which works at the level of the transfer matrix (not the character expansion).\n\nThe Lüscher transfer matrix T = V^{1/2} · U · V^{1/2} where:\n- V is a multiplication operator (from spatial plaquettes).\n- U is an integral operator (from temporal plaquettes).\n\nThe key is that U is a PD integral operator, which means its kernel K(x, y) satisfies `∫∫ f(x)·conj(f(y))·K(x, y) dx dy ≥ 0`. This is a DOUBLE integral (PD kernel), not a single integral.\n\nThe transfer matrix inner product `⟨g, Tg⟩ = ⟨g, V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}·g⟩ = ⟨V^{1/2}g, U·V^{1/2}g⟩ = ∫∫ (V^{1/2}g)(x)·conj((V^{1/2}g)(y))·K(x, y) dx dy ≥ 0`.\n\nSo the Lüscher decomposition converts the SINGLE integral `⟨g, Tg⟩` into a DOUBLE integral (PD kernel) by splitting T into V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}.\n\nThe key is that U is a PD integral operator. This means the kernel K(x, y) of U is a PD function, i.e., `∫∫ f(x)·conj(f(y))·K(x, y) dx dy ≥ 0`.\n\nNow, in our case, the transfer matrix T integrates out V⁺ (the positive config). The kernel of T is:\n`K_T(u⁺, u⁰, V⁺, u⁰') = exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺)/2) · exp(-β·S_int(u⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) · δ(u⁰', σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺)/2)`\n\nWait, this is getting complicated. Let me think about the Lüscher decomposition more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the Lüscher decomposition for the Wilson action works as follows:\n\nThe transfer matrix at time n evolves from slice n to slice n+1. It integrates out the temporal links at time n. The state is the spatial links at each slice.\n\nAt the interface (time 0), the transfer matrix T_0 integrates out w (temporal links at time 0). The state before is the spatial links at time 0 (u⁰_s), and the state after is the spatial links at time 1 (which are positive links).\n\nThe kernel of T_0 is:\n`K_0(u⁰_s, u¹_s) = ∫_w exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u¹, u⁰_s, w)) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) dμ_w`\n\nWait, this doesn't look right either. Let me think about the transfer matrix more carefully.\n\nActually, the transfer matrix in the Lüscher formulation is:\n`(Tg)(u⁰_s) = ∫ K_0(u⁰_s, u¹_s) · g(u¹_s) dμ(u¹_s)`\n\nwhere K_0 is the kernel integrating out the temporal links w.\n\nBut in our case, the transfer matrix T is defined differently — it integrates out V⁺ (the full positive config), not just w. So T is a COMPOSED transfer matrix (product of single-slice transfer matrices).\n\nThe Lüscher decomposition would decompose T into a product of single-slice transfer matrices, each of which is positive. But this requires a different formalization than what's currently in the code.\n\nOK, I think I've analyzed this enough. The key takeaway is:\n\n**The Lüscher decomposition (T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}) is the cleanest path, but it requires decomposing the transfer matrix into single-slice transfer matrices, which is a different formalization than the current character expansion approach.**\n\nThe current approach (character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor) leads to the reorganization obstacle. The Lüscher approach (decomposing the transfer matrix into single-slice transfer matrices) avoids this by working at the operator level.\n\nFor the next session, I recommend:\n1. Prove the identity `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)` formally.\n2. Consider the Lüscher decomposition approach: decompose the transfer matrix into single-slice transfer matrices.\n3. Show each single-slice transfer matrix is positive (using the PD property of the plaquette factor).\n4. Conclude the full transfer matrix is positive.\n\nThis is a significant change in approach from the character expansion, but it might be cleaner.\n\nLet me document this and prepare the handoff.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The Lüscher approach requires decomposing the transfer matrix into single-slice transfer matrices, which is a LOT of new infrastructure. The character expansion approach is already mostly done (the fullReflect form is proven). The only missing piece is the reorganization.\n\nMaybe I should think about whether there's a way to make the reorganization work with a MORE GENERAL lemma.\n\nThe `reflection_positivity_reorganization` lemma requires `d_w(x)·conj(d_w(y))` — the SAME d for both. The fullReflect form has `A_{w*}(σ(u⁰))·A_w(u⁰)` — DIFFERENT functions.\n\nBut what if we could show that `A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) = conj(A_w(u⁰))` for real f? Then the fullReflect form would become `conj(A_w(u⁰))·A_w(u⁰) = |A_w(u⁰)|²`, and the reorganization would work.\n\nIs `A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) = conj(A_w(u⁰))` for real f? Let me think.\n\n`A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w, V⁺) dμ⁺`\n\n`A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w*, V⁺) dμ⁺`\n\nFor real ψ, `conj(A_w(u⁰)) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺)) dμ⁺ = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(w*, V⁺) dμ⁺` (using conj(χ_λ) = χ_{dual(λ)}).\n\nWait, is `conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺)) = charFactorPos(w*, V⁺)`? Let me check.\n\n`charFactorPos(w, V⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w(l)}(V⁺(l))`. And `conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺)) = ∏_{l ∈ pos} conj(χ_{w(l)}(V⁺(l))) = ∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{dual(w(l))}(V⁺(l))` (using `conj(χ_λ(g)) = χ_{dual(λ)}(g)`).\n\nAnd `charFactorPos(w*, V⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ pos} χ_{w*(l)}(V⁺(l))`. Now, w*(l) for pos links is `dual(w(φ(l)))` if time-like, `w(φ(l))` if spatial. This is NOT the same as `dual(w(l))` (which is what we'd need for `conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺)) = charFactorPos(w*, V⁺)`).\n\nSo `conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺)) ≠ charFactorPos(w*, V⁺)` in general. The fullReflect reindexing w* involves the REFLECTION φ (swapping pos↔neg), not just the dual.\n\nSo `conj(A_w(u⁰)) ≠ A_{w*}(σ(u⁰))` in general. This is the fundamental obstacle.\n\nBUT — what if we DON'T use the fullReflect reindexing, and instead use a SIMPLER reindexing? What if w* = dual ∘ w (just apply dual to each link, without the reflection φ)?\n\nThen `conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺)) = charFactorPos(dual∘w, V⁺)`. And `conj(A_w(u⁰)) = ∫ ψ(merge(V⁺, u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorPos(dual∘w, V⁺) dμ⁺ = A_{dual∘w}(u⁰)`.\n\nSo `conj(A_w(u⁰)) = A_{dual∘w}(u⁰)`. This is a clean relationship!\n\nNow, the fullReflect form (before the fullReflect substitution) is:\n`C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · B_w(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n\nwhere `B_w(u⁰) = fourierCoeffNeg(dual∘w, u⁰)` (the negative Fourier coefficient).\n\nAnd `B_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorNeg(dual∘w, reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)) dμ⁺`.\n\nHmm, this is the form BEFORE the fullReflect substitution. The fullReflect substitution converts B_w to A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)).\n\nBut what if we DON'T do the fullReflect substitution, and instead use the relationship `conj(A_w(u⁰)) = A_{dual∘w}(u⁰)`?\n\nThe form is:\n`C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} charFactorInt(w, u⁰) · B_w(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n\nNow, `B_w(u⁰) = fourierCoeffNeg(dual∘w, u⁰)`. And from the `star_charFactorNeg_eq_charFactorPos_fullReflect` lemma, `star(charFactorNeg(dual∘w, reflectPosToNeg(V⁺))) = charFactorPos(w*, V⁺)`.\n\nHmm, this is the fullReflect identity. Let me think about whether there's a SIMPLER identity.\n\nActually, let me think about what `B_w(u⁰)` is in terms of `A`. The negative Fourier coefficient is:\n`B_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(merge(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) · exp(-β·S⁺/2) · charFactorNeg(dual∘w, reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)) dμ⁺`\n\nAnd `charFactorNeg(dual∘w, reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)) = ∏_{l ∈ neg} χ_{dual(w(l))}(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)(l))`.\n\nNow, `reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)` is the negative config obtained by reflecting V⁺. The negative links of `reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)` are the reflected positive links of V⁺.\n\nUnder reflection, a positive link at (n, μ) maps to a negative link at (θn, μ) with value V⁺(n, μ)⁻¹ if μ=0 (temporal), V⁺(n, μ) if μ≠0 (spatial).\n\nSo `χ_{dual(w(l))}(reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)(l)) = χ_{dual(w(l))}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l))⁻¹)` if l is temporal, `χ_{dual(w(l))}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l)))` if l is spatial.\n\nAnd `χ_{dual(λ)}(g⁻¹) = conj(χ_{dual(λ)}(g)) = χ_λ(g)` (using `conj(χ_{dual(λ)}) = χ_λ` and `χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g))`).\n\nWait, `χ_λ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ_λ(g))` (since χ_λ(g⁻¹) = Tr(ρ_λ(g⁻¹)) = Tr(ρ_λ(g)†) = conj(Tr(ρ_λ(g))) = conj(χ_λ(g))). And `χ_{dual(λ)}(g) = conj(χ_λ(g))` (by the hdual hypothesis). So `χ_{dual(λ)}(g⁻¹) = conj(χ_{dual(λ)}(g)) = conj(conj(χ_λ(g))) = χ_λ(g)`.\n\nSo for temporal links: `χ_{dual(w(l))}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l))⁻¹) = χ_{w(l)}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l)))`.\nAnd for spatial links: `χ_{dual(w(l))}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l))) = conj(χ_{w(l)}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l))))`.\n\nSo `charFactorNeg(dual∘w, reflectPosToNeg(V⁺)) = [∏_{temporal neg} χ_{w(l)}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l)))] · [∏_{spatial neg} conj(χ_{w(l)}(V⁺(φ⁻¹(l))))]`.\n\nThis is NOT simply `conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺))` because:\n- For temporal links: we get `χ_{w(l)}` (NOT conj).\n- For spatial links: we get `conj(χ_{w(l)})`.\n\nWhile `conj(charFactorPos(w, V⁺)) = ∏_{pos} conj(χ_{w(l)}(V⁺(l))) = ∏_{pos} χ_{dual(w(l))}(V⁺(l))`.\n\nThese are different because the fullReflect involves the reflection φ (swapping pos↔neg) and the dual/temporal inversion.\n\nOK, this is getting very complicated. I think the fundamental issue is that the fullReflect reindexing is NOT simply `dual∘w`, and this prevents the clean `conj(A_w) = A_{w*}` relationship.\n\nLet me step back and think about what's the most practical path forward.\n\nI think the most practical path is:\n1. Prove the identity `∫ G·G(θU) = ∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S)` formally.\n2. This gives a cleaner starting point: `∫ f·f(θU)·exp(-β·S) ≥ 0`.\n3. Then work on proving this using either the temporal expansion or the Lüscher decomposition.\n\nFor the next session, I recommend starting with step 1 (the identity) and then exploring the Lüscher decomposition approach.\n\nLet me document this and prepare the handoff. I'm at 83% context, so I should hand off soon.\n\nLet me write the design doc section and update the task lists.","turn":35,"is_prune_marker":false}
